            "source_section": "Definition",
            "source_excerpt": "Let $P$ be a set of primitive type labels. A formal type $\\Gamma$ records a finitely supported amount function $\\nu_\\Gamma:P\\to\\mathbb R_{\\ge0}$, not identically zero. Primitive generators have their unit amount function. Positive scaling, separated product, and thermal-join constructors retain distinct formal tags and obey\n\n$$\\nu_{s\\Gamma}=s\\nu_\\Gamma,\\qquad \\nu_{\\Gamma\\otimes\\Gamma'}=\\nu_\\Gamma+\\nu_{\\Gamma'}\\qquad(s>0).$$\n\nSeparated and joined types are distinct constructors even when their amount functions agree. Permutation, reassociation, and scaled-splitting conventions preserve the amount function. Define the positive **bookkeeping weight** $w_\\Gamma:=\\sum_{p\\in P}\\nu_\\Gamma(p)$, a finite sum over support. Thus $w_{s\\Gamma}=s w_\\Gamma$ and every product or join has weight $w_\\Gamma+w_{\\Gamma'}$. The optional empty unit has zero amount function and weight zero; it is represented only by the empty compound, not as a simple chart. These are formal amounts, not masses in kilograms or an empirical conservation law. The functions and pointwise operations use [[def-function-space]]; their finite-support convention and finite sums are those of [[def-tdmath-tagged-scalable-compound]] and [[lem-tdmath-compound-mass-well-defined]].\n\nFor Euclidean vectors use $a\\cdot b=\\sum_j a_jb_j$ and $\\|a\\|=(a\\cdot a)^{1/2}$. Write $B(a,r)=\\{b:\\|b-a\\|<r\\}$. Open means each point has such a ball contained in the set; convex means $(1-t)a+tb$ belongs to the set for all its $a,b$ and $0\\le t\\le1$. In a subspace or subset, use intersections of these balls for the relative topology. A map $P:D\\to\\mathbb R^n$ is locally Lipschitz if each $a\\in D$ has a relative neighborhood $V$ and finite $L\\ge0$ with $\\|P(b)-P(c)\\|\\le L\\|b-c\\|$ for all $b,c\\in V$. A set $D$ is arcwise connected here if each two distinct points are joined by a continuous injective map $\\gamma:[0,1]\\to D$ with those endpoints; singleton