          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "Let $C$ be a nonempty convex set in a finite-dimensional affine Euclidean space $H$ of dimension $k\\ge1$, let $\\operatorname{aff}C=H$, and put $K=\\overline{C}^{\\,H}$. Boundary empty: The item makes no empty boundary claim; this case does not apply to its stated result.",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Statement",
            "source_excerpt": "For nonempty $C$, define $\\operatorname{aff}C=\\{\\sum_{j=1}^N a_jc_j:N\\ge1,\\ c_j\\in C,\\ a_j\\in\\mathbb R,\\ \\sum_j a_j=1\\}$. An affine Euclidean space is a translate of a finite-dimensional real linear subspace with the induced Euclidean metric. A supporting hyperplane at $z$ is $\\{q:n\\cdot(q-z)=0\\}$ for a nonzero vector $n$, with $z$ in the set and all its points in one closed half-space. Let $C$ be a nonempty convex set in a finite-dimensional affine Euclidean space $H$ of dimension $k\\ge1$, let $\\operatorname{aff}C=H$, and put $K=\\overline{C}^{\\,H}$. If $z\\in\\partial_H K$ (that is, $z\\in K$ and every relative neighborhood of $z$ meets $H\\setminus K$), then there is a nonzero linear functional $\\ell$ on the translation space of $H$ such that $\\ell(q-z)\\le0$ for all $q\\in C$. If $C\\subset\\mathbb R^d$ has affine dimension at most $d-2$, then every $z\\in C$ has at least two distinct ambient supporting hyperplanes containing $\\operatorname{aff}C$.",
