          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "Let $K\\subset\\mathbb R^d$ be closed and convex with nonempty interior, $d\\ge1$, and let $x\\in\\partial K$. Boundary empty: The item makes no empty boundary claim; this case does not apply to its stated result.",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Statement",
            "source_excerpt": "Use the Euclidean distance $\\|q-z\\|$; $\\operatorname{int}K$ consists of points containing a ball in $K$, closure consists of points whose every ball meets $K$, and $\\partial K=\\overline K\\setminus\\operatorname{int}K$. Closed means equal to closure. A supporting hyperplane at $x\\in K$ is $\\{q:n\\cdot(q-x)=0\\}$ with $n\\ne0$ and $K$ contained in one closed half-space. Let $K\\subset\\mathbb R^d$ be closed and convex with nonempty interior, $d\\ge1$, and let $x\\in\\partial K$. Suppose $K$ has exactly one supporting hyperplane at $x$. Choose its inward unit normal $n$, so $n\\cdot(q-x)\\ge0$ for every $q\\in K$. Then for every $v$ with $n\\cdot v>0$, there is $\\delta>0$ such that $x+tv\\in\\operatorname{int}K$ for every $0<t<\\delta$.",
