          "id": "step-1-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "Choose a closed cube centered at $x_0$ whose concentric dilation by factor $2$ lies in $U$. Every point of the cube is a convex combination of its finitely many vertices, so [F1] and convexity bound $f$ above there by the largest vertex value $M$. If $x$ lies in the cube, then $2x_0-x$ lies in the dilated cube; convexity gives $f(x_0)\\le(f(x)+f(2x_0-x))/2$, hence $f(x)\\ge2f(x_0)-M'$, where $M'$ is the largest value of $f$ at the finitely many vertices of the dilated cube, an upper bound there by the same convex-combination argument. Thus $f$ is bounded above and below on a ball about $x_0$.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "Choose concentric balls $B(x_0,r)\\subset B(x_0,2r)\\subset U$ on which $m\\le f\\le M$. For distinct $x,y\\in B(x_0,r/2)$, extend the segment from $x$ through $y$ to $z=y+(r/2)(y-x)/\\|y-x\\|$, which lies in $B(x_0,2r)$. Put $\\theta=\\|y-x\\|/(r/2+\\|y-x\\|)$, so $y=(1-\\theta)x+\\theta z$. Convexity yields $f(y)-f(x)\\le\\theta(M-m)\\le(2(M-m)/r)\\|y-x\\|$. Repeating with $x,y$ interchanged gives the reverse bound. Negation proves the concave case.",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
