            "source_excerpt": "Use the Euclidean norm $\\|z\\|=(\\sum_{j=1}^d z_j^2)^{1/2}$, and balls $B(a,r)=\\{z:\\|z-a\\|<r\\}$. A set is open if it contains a ball about each of its points. Let $U\\subset\\mathbb R^d$ be open and convex, and let $f:U\\to\\mathbb R$ be finite and convex, meaning $f((1-t)x+ty)\\le(1-t)f(x)+tf(y)$ for every $x,y\\in U$ and $t\\in[0,1]$. For every $x_0\\in U$, there are a neighborhood $V$ of $x_0$ and $L<\\infty$ such that $|f(x)-f(y)|\\le L\\|x-y\\|$ for all $x,y\\in V$. The same conclusion holds for finite concave functions by applying the result to $-f$.",
