          "reason": "Let $u,v$ be finite-support real coefficient maps on the tagged state labels of [[def-tdmath-tagged-scalable-compound]] with equal total coefficient. Boundary one: The item makes no one boundary claim; this case does not apply to its stated result.",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Definition",
            "source_excerpt": "Let $u,v$ be finite-support real coefficient maps on the tagged state labels of [[def-tdmath-tagged-scalable-compound]] with equal total coefficient. For a typed state $x$, compound notation uses the represented map $\\mathsf{J}(x)$ from [[def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses]]; its raw unit point mass is $e_{(\\Gamma,x)}$. Signed maps still use the ordinary total of their raw coefficients, exactly the bookkeeping mass of the represented nonnegative maps. Put $d=v-u$, $d^+(x)=\\max(d(x),0)$ and $d^-(x)=\\max(-d(x),0)$. Define $u\\preceq_s v$ to mean $d^-\\prec d^+$ under the compound relation of [[def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses]]. Write $u\\sim_s v$ when both $u\\preceq_s v$ and $v\\preceq_s u$. These are formal signed comparison maps, not physical negative amounts. The positive and negative parts are finite-support compounds of equal mass. If $u\\sim_s v$, then $cu\\sim_s cv$ for every real $c$: positive $c$ uses scaling, $c=0$ is reflexivity, and negative $c$ swaps the positive and negative parts while equivalence supplies both comparison directions. This Definition introduces the signed notation; ordinary-order compatibility and the signed-order laws are proved separately.",
