        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-tdmath-relative-boundary-support",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For nonempty $C$, define $\\operatorname{aff}C=\\{\\sum_{j=1}^N a_jc_j:N\\ge1,\\ c_j\\in C,\\ a_j\\in\\mathbb R,\\ \\sum_j a_j=1\\}$. An affine Euclidean space is a translate of a finite-dimensional real linear subspace with the induced Euclidean metric. A supporting hyperplane at $z$ is $\\{q:n\\cdot(q-z)=0\\}$ for a nonzero vector $n$, with $z$ in the set and all its points in one closed half-space. Let $C$ be a nonempty convex set in a finite-dimensional affine Euclidean space $H$ of dimension $k\\ge1$, let $\\operatorname{aff}C=H$, and put $K=\\overline{C}^{\\,H}$. If $z\\in\\partial_H K$ (that is, $z\\in K$ and every relative neighborhood of $z$ meets $H\\setminus K$), then there is a nonzero linear functional $\\ell$ on the translation space of $H$ such that $\\ell(q-z)\\le0$ for all $q\\in C$. If $C\\subset\\mathbb R^d$ has affine dimension at most $d-2$, then every $z\\in C$ has at least two distinct ambient supporting hyperplanes containing $\\operatorname{aff}C$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-tdmath-relative-interior-segment",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For a nonempty set $C$, define $\\operatorname{aff}C=\\{\\sum_{j=1}^N a_jc_j:N\\ge1,\\ c_j\\in C,\\ a_j\\in\\mathbb R,\\ \\sum_j a_j=1\\}$. With a fixed $c_0\\in C$, this is $c_0+\\operatorname{span}\\{c-c_0:c\\in C\\}$, where span consists of finite linear combinations. An affine Euclidean space is such a translate of a finite-dimensional linear subspace with its induced Euclidean distance. Relative balls use this distance; interior means containing a relative ball, and closure means every relative ball meets the set. Let $C$ be a nonempty convex subset of a finite-dimensional affine Euclidean space $H$. Its relative interior $\\operatorname{ri}_H C$ is the interior in $H=\\operatorname{aff}C$. Then $\\operatorname{ri}_H C$ is nonempty. If $w\\in\\operatorname{ri}_H C$, $y\\in\\overline{C}^{\\,H}$, and $0\\le t<1$, then $(1-t)w+ty\\in\\operatorname{ri}_H C$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.3"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Suppose $A_x$ has empty ambient interior. If $d=1$, S1 supplies a strict successor $y\\ne x$; convexity puts the nondegenerate interval between $x$ and $y$ in $A_x$, contradicting empty interior. Thus take $d\\ge2$. The affine hull of $A_x$ has dimension at most $d-1$. If its dimension were at most $d-2$, [F2] would give two distinct supporting hyperplanes at $x$, contradicting S2. Hence its affine hull is a hyperplane $H$; this $H$ supports $A_x$ at every point, including $x$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "By S1 choose $y$ with $x\\prec\\prec y$. Then $y\\in A_x$ and $x\\notin A_y$. Transitivity gives $A_y\\subseteq A_x\\subseteq H$. If $A_y$ had affine dimension at most $d-2$, [F2] would again give multiple ambient supports at $y$, contrary to S2. Thus $\\operatorname{aff}A_y=H$.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-3",
          "claim": "Choose $w\\in\\operatorname{ri}_H A_y$ by [F3]. The segment from $w$ to $x$ is contained in $H\\cap\\Gamma_i$ by convexity of both sets, starts in $A_y$, and ends outside $A_y$. Its intersection with $A_y$ is closed by relative closedness [F1], so it has a last point $z$ before $x$. Points immediately after $z$ on this segment lie outside $A_y$ and, because $A_y$ is closed in the open set $\\Gamma_i$, outside $\\overline{A_y}^{\\,H}$. Thus $z\\in\\partial_H\\overline{A_y}^{\\,H}$. The support statement [F2] supplies a nonzero functional on $H$ whose zero hyperplane supports $A_y$ at $z$.",
          "step": "1.3",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "choose"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Extend this functional to the ambient Euclidean space by taking its normal vector in the direction space of $H$. Its zero hyperplane contains the within-$H$ support and is not $H$ itself. Since $z\\in A_y$, transitivity gives $A_z\\subseteq A_y$, so the extended plane supports $A_z$ at $z$; the plane $H$ also supports $A_z$. These are distinct, contradicting S2 at $z$. Therefore $A_x$ has nonempty ambient interior.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "step 1.3",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
