            "8.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-tdmath-convex-sector-closedness",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Under [[def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses]], for every simple state $x$, the forward sector $A_x=\\{y\\in\\Gamma_i:x\\prec y\\}$ is convex and closed relative to $\\Gamma_i$. For collinear $y=tx+(1-t)z$, $0<t<1$, $y\\prec z$ implies $x\\prec y$, and $x\\prec z$ implies $x\\prec y$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "6.1",
            "7.1",
            "8.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-tdmath-sector-interior",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Under [[def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses]], every forward sector $A_x$ has nonempty interior in its ambient simple domain $\\Gamma_i$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "4.1",
            "6.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-tdmath-relative-boundary-support",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For nonempty $C$, define $\\operatorname{aff}C=\\{\\sum_{j=1}^N a_jc_j:N\\ge1,\\ c_j\\in C,\\ a_j\\in\\mathbb R,\\ \\sum_j a_j=1\\}$. An affine Euclidean space is a translate of a finite-dimensional real linear subspace with the induced Euclidean metric. A supporting hyperplane at $z$ is $\\{q:n\\cdot(q-z)=0\\}$ for a nonzero vector $n$, with $z$ in the set and all its points in one closed half-space. Let $C$ be a nonempty convex set in a finite-dimensional affine Euclidean space $H$ of dimension $k\\ge1$, let $\\operatorname{aff}C=H$, and put $K=\\overline{C}^{\\,H}$. If $z\\in\\partial_H K$ (that is, $z\\in K$ and every relative neighborhood of $z$ meets $H\\setminus K$), then there is a nonzero linear functional $\\ell$ on the translation space of $H$ such that $\\ell(q-z)\\le0$ for all $q\\in C$. If $C\\subset\\mathbb R^d$ has affine dimension at most $d-2$, then every $z\\in C$ has at least two distinct ambient supporting hyperplanes containing $\\operatorname{aff}C$.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1",
            "6.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-tdmath-unique-support-inward-ray",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Use the Euclidean distance $\\|q-z\\|$; $\\operatorname{int}K$ consists of points containing a ball in $K$, closure consists of points whose every ball meets $K$, and $\\partial K=\\overline K\\setminus\\operatorname{int}K$. Closed means equal to closure. A supporting hyperplane at $x\\in K$ is $\\{q:n\\cdot(q-x)=0\\}$ with $n\\ne0$ and $K$ contained in one closed half-space. Let $K\\subset\\mathbb R^d$ be closed and convex with nonempty interior, $d\\ge1$, and let $x\\in\\partial K$. Suppose $K$ has exactly one supporting hyperplane at $x$. Choose its inward unit normal $n$, so $n\\cdot(q-x)\\ge0$ for every $q\\in K$. Then for every $v$ with $n\\cdot v>0$, there is $\\delta>0$ such that $x+tv\\in\\operatorname{int}K$ for every $0<t<\\delta$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "4.1",
            "6.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "lem-tdmath-convex-function-local-lipschitz",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Use the Euclidean norm $\\|z\\|=(\\sum_{j=1}^d z_j^2)^{1/2}$, and balls $B(a,r)=\\{z:\\|z-a\\|<r\\}$. A set is open if it contains a ball about each of its points. Let $U\\subset\\mathbb R^d$ be open and convex, and let $f:U\\to\\mathbb R$ be finite and convex, meaning $f((1-t)x+ty)\\le(1-t)f(x)+tf(y)$ for every $x,y\\in U$ and $t\\in[0,1]$. For every $x_0\\in U$, there are a neighborhood $V$ of $x_0$ and $L<\\infty$ such that $|f(x)-f(y)|\\le L\\|x-y\\|$ for all $x,y\\in V$. The same conclusion holds for finite concave functions by applying the result to $-f$.",
          "uses": [
            "7.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "lem-tdmath-integral-zero-uniqueness",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Use the Darboux integral on nondegenerate intervals and the convention $\\int_a^a f=0$. Let $L\\ge0$, $T>0$, and let $f:[0,T]\\to[0,\\infty)$ be continuous. If $f(t)\\le L\\int_0^t f(s)\\,ds$ for every $t\\in[0,T]$, then $f(t)=0$ for every $t\\in[0,T]$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "thm-heine-borel-rn",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$ with $n \\ge 1$, let $\\mathbb{R}^n$ be the set of functions\n$n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ and let $d_2$ be the Euclidean metric on it\n([[lem-metrics-on-rn]]). Then:\n\n1. **Closed boxes are compact.** For reals $a_k \\le b_k$ $(k < n)$ the box\n   $Q = \\{\\, x \\in \\mathbb{R}^n : a_k \\le x_k \\le b_k \\text{ for every } k < n \\,\\}$\n   is a compact subset of $(\\mathbb{R}^n, d_2)$ ([[def-metric-compactness]]).\n2. **Heine-Borel.** A subset $K \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^n$ is a compact subset of\n   $(\\mathbb{R}^n, d_2)$ if and only if $K$ is closed in $\\mathbb{R}^n$\n   ([[def-metric-topology]]) and bounded\n   ([[def-metric-bounded-diameter]]).\n3. **The real line.** A subset $K \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ is a compact subset of\n   $(\\mathbb{R}, d_{\\mathbb{R}})$, the usual metric $d_{\\mathbb{R}}(x,y) = |x-y|$\n   ([[lem-real-line-is-a-metric-space]]), if and only if $K$ is closed in\n   $\\mathbb{R}$ and bounded.\n\n**No choice principle is used.** The bisection below halves one coordinate at a\ntime and takes the **left** half whenever the left half still fails to be finitely\ncovered, the right half otherwise: a rule with two outcomes, decided by a\nproperty of the box, not a selection. That is the whole reason the theorem is\navailable in ZF, while the general \"complete and totally bounded implies compact\"\n([[thm-complete-and-totally-bounded-implies-compact]]) is not.\n\nThe hypothesis $n \\ge 1$ is inherited from [[lem-metrics-on-rn]], which defines\n$\\mathbb{R}^n$ and its metrics only there; the last remark below records what\nhappens at $n = 0$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "def-total-derivative-in-euclidean-space",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^m$ be open, let $a\\in U$, and let $f:U\\to\\mathbb R^n$. The map $f$ is **totally differentiable at $a$** when there is a linear map $L:\\mathbb R^m\\to\\mathbb R^n$ ([[def-euclidean-linear-map]]) such that\n\n$$\\lim_{h\\to0}\\frac{\\|f(a+h)-f(a)-Lh\\|_2}{\\|h\\|_2}=0,$$\n\nwhere the quotient is considered for $h\\ne0$ with $a+h\\in U$. The map $L$, when it exists, is denoted $Df(a)$ and called the **total derivative**. Equivalently, $f(a+h)=f(a)+Df(a)h+r(h)$ with $\\|r(h)\\|_2/\\|h\\|_2\\to0$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "7.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $k\\in\\mathbb N$, let $m,q\\ge1$, let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^m$ be open, and let $f:U\\to\\mathbb R^q$. A map $f:U\\to\\mathbb R^q$ is of class $C^k$ when each component is of class $C^k$. Each scalar component uses the word-derivative convention of [[def-ck-and-multi-index-notation-in-several-variables]], whose source dimension is positive. The map is **smooth**, or $C^\\infty$, when it is $C^k$ for every $k\\in\\mathbb N$.\n\nLet $n\\ge1$ and let $U,V\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be open. A bijection $f:U\\to V$ is a **$C^k$ diffeomorphism** when both $f$ and $f^{-1}$ are $C^k$. For $k\\ge1$, a **local $C^k$ diffeomorphism at $a\\in U$** is a restriction $f|_P:P\\to Q$ between open neighbourhoods of $a$ and $f(a)$ that is a $C^k$ diffeomorphism. At $k=1$ this agrees with [[def-c-one-map-and-local-inverse]]: continuous first partial derivatives give the required total derivative by [[thm-continuous-partial-derivatives-imply-total-differentiability]], while continuity of the total derivative gives continuity of its matrix entries and hence of the first partial derivatives.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "7.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "thm-continuous-partial-derivatives-imply-total-differentiability",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^m$ be open and let $f:U\\to\\mathbb R^n$. Suppose every partial derivative $\\partial_jf$ exists on a neighbourhood of $a\\in U$ and is continuous at $a$. Then $f$ is totally differentiable at $a$, and $Df(a)$ is the linear map with matrix $Jf(a)$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "7.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $f:U\\to V\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be totally differentiable at $a\\in U$ and let $g:V\\to\\mathbb R^p$ be totally differentiable at $f(a)$. Then $g\\circ f$ is totally differentiable at $a$ and\n\n$$D(g\\circ f)(a)=Dg(f(a))\\circ Df(a).$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "7.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "thm-newton-leibniz-with-interior-derivative",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $a<b$. Suppose $G:[a,b]\\to\\mathbb R$ is continuous on $[a,b]$ and differentiable on $(a,b)$. If $f:[a,b]\\to\\mathbb R$ is Riemann integrable and\n\n$$f(x)=G'(x)\\qquad(a<x<b),$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\\int_a^b f=G(b)-G(a).$$\n\nNo derivative of $G$ at either endpoint is assumed, and the two endpoint values assigned to the integrable extension $f$ do not enter the conclusion.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F10",
          "source": "thm-continuous-implies-integrable",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $a < b$ be reals and let $f : [a,b] \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be continuous on $[a,b]$\n([[def-continuity-real]]). Then $f$ is bounded ([[def-bounded-set]]) and Riemann\nintegrable on $[a,b]$ ([[def-darboux-integral]]).\n\n**The proof gives more than integrability: it gives a partition that works.** For\nevery real $\\varepsilon > 0$ the uniform partition into $N$ parts already\nsatisfies $U(f,P) - L(f,P) < \\varepsilon$, as soon as $N$ is large enough that\n$(b-a)/\\iota(N)$ is below the $\\delta$ that uniform continuity supplies for\n$\\varepsilon/\\bigl(2(b-a)\\bigr)$. Uniform continuity is exactly what makes one\n$\\delta$ serve all $N$ subintervals at once, and it is the only place where the\ncompactness of $[a,b]$ is used.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "To apply [F4] to a sector at a boundary point $z$, choose $r>0$ with $\\overline B(z,r)\\subset\\Gamma_i$ and put $K=A_x\\cap\\overline B(z,r)$. This is globally closed and convex, and agrees with $A_x$ near $z$. It has nonempty interior: join $z$ to an interior point of $A_x$ and take points sufficiently close to $z$. Supporting planes at $z$ coincide for $K$ and $A_x$: a local supporting inequality extends along every segment from $z$ to a point of $A_x$. Any such plane also supports $A_z\\subseteq A_x$ at $z$, so S2 makes it unique. Thus [F4] applies to $K$ and its interior conclusions hold for $A_x$ locally. For fixed $x=(u_x,v_x)$, the vertical section $I_x:=\\{u:(u,v_x)\\in A_x\\}$ is a relatively closed convex interval by [F1]. The support plane at $x$ confines it to one side of $u_x$. Its inward normal has nonzero energy component, so [F4] gives a nontrivial interior vertical segment on that side.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "Let $u,w$ be two $C^1$ graph solutions of $Dq=-P_i(q,v)$ on a common open domain, and suppose $u(v_0)=w(v_0)$. Since $P_i$ is locally Lipschitz, choose a convex neighborhood of $(u(v_0),v_0)$ on which one Lipschitz constant $L$ works. Continuity of $u,w$ lets us choose a convex ball about $v_0$ whose closure lies in the common domain and whose two graph images stay in that neighborhood; take any $v$ in a smaller concentric ball. Put $d=v-v_0$ and $\\gamma(t)=v_0+td$ for $0\\le t\\le1$. By [F8], $r(t)=u(\\gamma(t))-w(\\gamma(t))$ is $C^1$ and\n$r'(t)=[-P_i(u(\\gamma(t)),\\gamma(t))+P_i(w(\\gamma(t)),\\gamma(t))]\\cdot d.$\nThe local Lipschitz bound for $P_i$ gives $|r'(t)|\\le L\\|d\\||r(t)|$. The derivative is continuous, hence integrable by [F10]; [F9] gives $r(t)=\\int_0^t r'(s)\\,ds$; hence $h(t):=|r(t)|\\le L\\|d\\|\\int_0^t h(s)\\,ds$. Apply [F6] on $[0,1]$ to obtain $h=0$. Thus $u=w$ near $v_0$. Along any polygonal path in the common open domain, the set of path parameters at which equality holds is closed by continuity and open by this local result; since the path interval is connected and the set is nonempty, equality holds along the whole path. Every open connected subset of Euclidean space is polygonally connected: points reachable from a fixed point by finitely many segments in small balls form a nonempty relatively open and relatively closed set. Therefore equality extends throughout each common connected domain.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F6",
            "F8",
            "F9",
            "F10",
            "given",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "The convex, relatively closed interval $I_x$ contains a nontrivial segment on the inward side by step 1.1 and [F4]. Suppose it does not contain the whole open $\\Gamma_i$-fiber on that side. Its endpoint $b$ toward that side then lies inside $\\Gamma_i$; relative closedness puts $b$ in $I_x$, so the interval from $x$ to $b$ is a compact nondegenerate interval by [F7]. Let $x_1$ be its midpoint. Since $x_1\\in A_x$, transitivity gives $A_{x_1}\\subseteq A_x$. The supporting plane of $A_{x_1}$ has nonzero energy coefficient, so its vertical section lies wholly on one side of $x_1$; being contained in $I_x$, it lies in one half of $[x,b]$ and has at most half its length. It is nonempty and closed relative to $[x,b]$, hence compact. Repeat: take the midpoint $x_{k+1}$ of the current compact interval $I_{x_k}$; then $x_k\\prec x_{k+1}$, so the next section is nested inside the current one and again has at most half its length. These intervals have nonempty total intersection: otherwise their open complements would cover the first compact interval, a finite subcover would do so by [F7], and nesting would make one interval empty. Their lengths tend to zero, so the intersection has a unique point $x_\\infty$, still in the first interval contained in $\\Gamma_i$. The midpoint recursion and this unique intersection require no selection from an arbitrary countable family. Relative closedness puts $x_\\infty$ in every $A_{x_k}$, hence $A_{x_\\infty}\\subseteq A_{x_k}$ by transitivity. Its vertical section is therefore only $\\{x_\\infty\\}$, contradicting the nontrivial interior vertical segment at $x_\\infty$ supplied by [F4]. Thus $I_x$ contains the whole open fiber on its inward side.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F4",
            "F7",
            "step 1.1",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "All states on a fixed work-coordinate fiber have the same orientation. If $x$ is oriented upward and a state $z$ above it were oriented downward, the full upward section from step 2.1 gives $x\\prec z$, and the full downward section from $z$ gives $z\\prec w$ for a state $w$ below $x$, contradicting the support at $x$. If a state $y$ below an upward $x$ were oriented downward, choose $z$ above $x$ with $x\\prec z$; the first collinear implication in [F1], applied to $x$ between $y$ and $z$, gives $y\\prec x$, contradicting the downward support at $y$. The reversed argument handles a downward $x$.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "By step 3.1, orientation is constant on each fixed-work fiber. The fiber orientation is locally constant in $v$. At any $x$, choose one vertical point just inside the supported half-space and one just outside; by [F4] the inside point can be chosen in $\\operatorname{int}A_x$. Openness of $\\Gamma_i$ and of the interior and exterior regions preserves these memberships on nearby fibers. Each such fiber therefore meets $\\partial A_x$ between these two energy values. A support of $A_x$ at that boundary point also supports the point's own sector and so has its S2 orientation; the inside/outside placement fixes that orientation. Thus nearby fibers have the same sign as the fiber through $x$. Since $\\Gamma_i$ is convex, it is connected; a locally constant two-valued sign is constant.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "step 3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-5-1",
          "claim": "Adopt the coordinate convention $\\varepsilon_i=1$. Step 2.1 and the common orientation give $x\\prec y$ exactly for $u_y\\ge u_x$ at fixed $v$, with strict comparison for $u_y>u_x$.",
          "step": "5.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 3.1",
            "step 4.1",
            "algebra",
            "step 2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-6-1",
          "claim": "Put $\\rho_x=\\pi_v(\\partial A_x)$. At a boundary point $z\\in\\partial A_x$, [F3] supplies a support to $A_x$. Because $A_z\\subseteq A_x$, that plane also supports $A_z$ and equals its unique S2 plane. Its finite slope gives a nonvertical inward direction; [F4] supplies nearby vertical points inside $A_x$, while the opposite side is outside by the support. Neighborhoods of one inside and one outside point show every nearby work fiber meets $\\partial A_x$. Hence $\\rho_x$ is open. It is connected because S3 makes $\\partial A_x$ arcwise connected and projection is continuous. By step 5.1, every vertical fiber over $\\rho_x$ has exactly one boundary energy; call it $u_x(v)$.",
          "step": "6.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "step 5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-7-1",
          "claim": "For $v,v'\\in\\rho_x$, convexity of $A_x$ puts every convex combination of their boundary states in $A_x$. On any sufficiently small convex ball in $\\rho_x$, the unique-boundary property and upward orientation make $u_x$ the least energy in each fiber. Therefore $u_x(tv+(1-t)v')\\le t u_x(v)+(1-t)u_x(v')$, so $u_x$ is finite and convex there. It is locally Lipschitz by [F5]. The inward support at $(u_x(v),v)$ gives\n$u_x(v')-u_x(v)\\ge -P_i(u_x(v),v)\\cdot(v'-v).$\nThe support at $(u_x(v'),v')$ gives\n$u_x(v')-u_x(v)\\le -P_i(u_x(v'),v')\\cdot(v'-v).$\nLocal Lipschitz continuity of $u_x$ and $P_i$ makes the difference between these linear bounds $O(\\|v'-v\\|^2)$. Thus $u_x$ is differentiable with derivative $-P_i(u_x(v),v)$, and that derivative is locally Lipschitz. Hence $u_x\\in C^1$ with locally Lipschitz derivative.",
          "step": "7.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F5",
            "F8",
            "step 6.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-8-1",
          "claim": "If $y\\in\\partial A_x$, then $A_y\\subseteq A_x$. The boundary graphs agree at $v_y$ and solve the same differential equation. The set of $v\\in\\rho_y$ where $v\\in\\rho_x$ and $u_y(v)=u_x(v)$ is nonempty, open by step 1.2, and closed by continuity and relative closedness of $\\partial A_x$. Connectedness of $\\rho_y$ makes it all of $\\rho_y$. This shows $\\rho_y\\subseteq\\rho_x$. The same argument, using relative closedness of $\\partial A_y$, shows $\\rho_y$ is relatively closed in connected $\\rho_x$; it is relatively open because both projections are open. Hence $\\rho_x=\\rho_y$ and the boundary graphs agree. In particular $x\\in\\partial A_y$, so $y\\prec x$; together with $y\\in A_x$ and transitivity this gives $A_x=A_y$.",
          "step": "8.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "step 7.1",
            "step 1.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
