        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-tdmath-forward-boundary-graph",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Under [[def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses]], each simple domain has one orientation sign $\\varepsilon_i\\in\\{-1,1\\}$, constant across the domain, such that at fixed work coordinate $v$, $x\\prec y$ exactly when $\\varepsilon_i(u_y-u_x)\\ge0$. In coordinates oriented so $\\varepsilon_i=1$, for each $x\\in\\Gamma_i$ the projection $\\rho_x$ of $\\partial_{\\Gamma_i}A_x$ onto work coordinates is open and connected; the boundary is the graph $(u_x(v),v)$ of a $C^1$ function with locally Lipschitz derivative\n\n$$Du_x(v)=-P_i(u_x(v),v).$$\n\nIf $y\\in\\partial A_x$, then $A_y=A_x$ and their boundary graphs agree.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-heine-borel-rn",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$ with $n \\ge 1$, let $\\mathbb{R}^n$ be the set of functions\n$n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ and let $d_2$ be the Euclidean metric on it\n([[lem-metrics-on-rn]]). Then:\n\n1. **Closed boxes are compact.** For reals $a_k \\le b_k$ $(k < n)$ the box\n   $Q = \\{\\, x \\in \\mathbb{R}^n : a_k \\le x_k \\le b_k \\text{ for every } k < n \\,\\}$\n   is a compact subset of $(\\mathbb{R}^n, d_2)$ ([[def-metric-compactness]]).\n2. **Heine-Borel.** A subset $K \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^n$ is a compact subset of\n   $(\\mathbb{R}^n, d_2)$ if and only if $K$ is closed in $\\mathbb{R}^n$\n   ([[def-metric-topology]]) and bounded\n   ([[def-metric-bounded-diameter]]).\n3. **The real line.** A subset $K \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ is a compact subset of\n   $(\\mathbb{R}, d_{\\mathbb{R}})$, the usual metric $d_{\\mathbb{R}}(x,y) = |x-y|$\n   ([[lem-real-line-is-a-metric-space]]), if and only if $K$ is closed in\n   $\\mathbb{R}$ and bounded.\n\n**No choice principle is used.** The bisection below halves one coordinate at a\ntime and takes the **left** half whenever the left half still fails to be finitely\ncovered, the right half otherwise: a rule with two outcomes, decided by a\nproperty of the box, not a selection. That is the whole reason the theorem is\navailable in ZF, while the general \"complete and totally bounded implies compact\"\n([[thm-complete-and-totally-bounded-implies-compact]]) is not.\n\nThe hypothesis $n \\ge 1$ is inherited from [[lem-metrics-on-rn]], which defines\n$\\mathbb{R}^n$ and its metrics only there; the last remark below records what\nhappens at $n = 0$.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "By T4, the open strips $\\Sigma(a,b):=\\{x:a\\prec\\prec x\\prec\\prec b\\}$ with $a\\prec\\prec b$ and $a\\sim_T b$ cover $\\Gamma_i$. They are open: by [F2], $a\\prec\\prec x$ is $x\\in\\operatorname{int}A_a$, and $x\\prec\\prec b$ is $x\\notin A_b$; the latter set is open because $A_b$ is relatively closed.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "A2",
            "F2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "Let $\\Sigma(a,b)$ and $\\Sigma(c,d)$ overlap. Their intersection is open. Choose two states $e\\prec\\prec f$ in a small vertical segment contained in the overlap, using [F4]. Let $s_e,s_f$ be their coordinates in the first strip and $t_e,t_f$ in the second, supplied by [F1]. If $s_f<s_e$, the signed difference $R_{s_e}-R_{s_f}=(s_e-s_f)(\\mathsf{J}(b)-\\mathsf{J}(a))$ and positive scaling and signed compatibility [A1, F3] give $R_{s_f}\\prec R_{s_e}$, so $f\\prec e$ by the two reference equivalences; if $s_f=s_e$, those references coincide and give $f\\sim e$. Both contradict $e\\prec\\prec f$. Hence $s_f>s_e$, and the same argument gives $t_f>t_e$.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "The four reference equivalences give, in signed comparison,\n$f-e\\sim_s(s_f-s_e)(b-a),\\qquad f-e\\sim_s(t_f-t_e)(d-c).$\nPut $r=(s_f-s_e)/(t_f-t_e)>0$. Additivity and positive scaling in [F3] give $d-c\\sim_s r(b-a)$. Applying the same algebra to the equivalences for $e$ gives $c\\sim_s a+(s_e-r t_e)(b-a)$. Consequently a state in the second strip with coordinate $t$ has first-chart coordinate $s_e+r(t-t_e)$. The change of chart is affine with strictly positive slope.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "step 1.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "The same calculation covers a finite chain of overlapping strips: after each overlap, express the new strip's endpoint states as signed-equivalent affine maps in the existing reference pair. The local strip lemma gives every state $z$ of the added strip a coordinate $s_z\\in\\mathbb R$ with $\\mathsf{J}(z)\\sim_s(1-s_z)\\mathsf{J}(a)+s_z\\mathsf{J}(b)$ in the existing chart. Signed addition, positive scaling, translation, and equivalence under negative scaling are valid by [F3]; hence induction along a finite overlap chain gives one such common chart for every state in the union.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "Take any finite list of target states in $\\Gamma_i$ and join them in order by finitely many straight segments, which lie in $\\Gamma_i$ by convexity. The resulting connected path is a finite union of closed bounded segments, hence compact by [F5]. The open strips from step 1.1 cover it, so compactness gives a finite subcover. The intersection graph of those strips meeting the path is connected: otherwise their unions would partition the connected path into two disjoint nonempty relatively open sets. Order a spanning tree from one root strip; each later strip overlaps an earlier one in an open set and can be glued by step 3.1. Thus every target state has a common signed chart $\\mathsf{J}(z)\\sim_s(1-s_z)\\mathsf{J}(a)+s_z\\mathsf{J}(b)$ with $a\\prec\\prec b$.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F5",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 3.1",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-5-1",
          "claim": "Write the arbitrary scaled-copy compounds as\n$$A=\\sum_j\\alpha_j\\mathsf{J}_{r_j\\Gamma_i}(r_jx_j),\\qquad B=\\sum_k\\beta_k\\mathsf{J}_{h_k\\Gamma_i}(h_ky_k),$$\nwith $r_j,h_k>0$ and nonnegative coefficients, omitting zero terms. The declared reversible transport in [A1] gives $\\mathsf{J}_{r_j\\Gamma_i}(r_jx_j)\\sim r_j\\mathsf{J}_{\\Gamma_i}(x_j)$ and similarly on the other side; ordinary positive scaling and composition therefore reduce them to the unscaled base-type states with effective amounts $a_j=\\alpha_jr_j$ and $b_k=\\beta_kh_k$. Their masses are respectively $r_jw_{\\Gamma_i}$ and $h_kw_{\\Gamma_i}$ before this transport, so equal total mass $M$ implies\n$$L=M/w_{\\Gamma_i}=\\sum_j a_j=\\sum_k b_k.$$\nIf $M=0$, both maps are empty and reflexivity applies. Otherwise use step 4.1 for the finite list of base states $x_j,y_k$, with common-chart coordinates $s_j,t_k$. Signed addition and splitting [F3] make the transported compounds equivalent to $R_{q_A}=(L-q_A)\\mathsf{J}(a)+q_A\\mathsf{J}(b)$ and $R_{q_B}=(L-q_B)\\mathsf{J}(a)+q_B\\mathsf{J}(b)$, where $q_A=\\sum_j a_js_j$ and $q_B=\\sum_k b_kt_k$. If $q_A<q_B$, their signed difference has positive part $(q_B-q_A)\\mathsf{J}(b)$ and negative part $(q_B-q_A)\\mathsf{J}(a)$, so $a\\prec b$ and positive scaling compare them; equality gives reflexivity, and $q_B<q_A$ gives the reverse comparison. Transitivity transfers this order through the reversible transports to the original distinct scaled tags. Hence CH holds for every allowed scaled-copy pair, without identifying those tags or dropping their scale factors.",
          "step": "5.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F3",
            "step 4.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
