            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-tdmath-additive-entropy-representation",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $X$ be nonempty and suppose its finite-support compounds carry the comparison structure of [[def-tdmath-comparison-and-stability]] over the complete ordered field $\\mathbb R$. If there are $x_0,x_1\\in X$ with $x_0\\preceq x_1$ and not $x_1\\preceq x_0$, define $S(x):=\\sup L_x$ using [[lem-tdmath-reference-supremum-bounds]]. Then $S:X\\to\\mathbb R$ is normalized by $S(x_0)=0$, $S(x_1)=1$, and for all equal-mass compounds $A=\\sum_i a_i e_{x_i}$ and $B=\\sum_j b_j e_{y_j}$,\n\n$$A\\preceq B\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\\sum_i a_iS(x_i)\\le\\sum_j b_jS(y_j).$$\n\nThus $S$ is an additive extensive representation on finite compounds. If no strict pair exists, CH instead makes every equal-mass pair of compounds equivalent, and any constant state function gives a (degenerate) additive representation. No concavity, continuity, or physical law follows without additional hypotheses.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.3",
            "2.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-tdmath-forward-boundary-graph",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Under [[def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses]], each simple domain has one orientation sign $\\varepsilon_i\\in\\{-1,1\\}$, constant across the domain, such that at fixed work coordinate $v$, $x\\prec y$ exactly when $\\varepsilon_i(u_y-u_x)\\ge0$. In coordinates oriented so $\\varepsilon_i=1$, for each $x\\in\\Gamma_i$ the projection $\\rho_x$ of $\\partial_{\\Gamma_i}A_x$ onto work coordinates is open and connected; the boundary is the graph $(u_x(v),v)$ of a $C^1$ function with locally Lipschitz derivative\n\n$$Du_x(v)=-P_i(u_x(v),v).$$\n\nIf $y\\in\\partial A_x$, then $A_y=A_x$ and their boundary graphs agree.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "1.3",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-tdmath-convex-function-local-lipschitz",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Use the Euclidean norm $\\|z\\|=(\\sum_{j=1}^d z_j^2)^{1/2}$, and balls $B(a,r)=\\{z:\\|z-a\\|<r\\}$. A set is open if it contains a ball about each of its points. Let $U\\subset\\mathbb R^d$ be open and convex, and let $f:U\\to\\mathbb R$ be finite and convex, meaning $f((1-t)x+ty)\\le(1-t)f(x)+tf(y)$ for every $x,y\\in U$ and $t\\in[0,1]$. For every $x_0\\in U$, there are a neighborhood $V$ of $x_0$ and $L<\\infty$ such that $|f(x)-f(y)|\\le L\\|x-y\\|$ for all $x,y\\in V$. The same conclusion holds for finite concave functions by applying the result to $-f$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "1.3",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-heine-borel-rn",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$ with $n \\ge 1$, let $\\mathbb{R}^n$ be the set of functions\n$n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ and let $d_2$ be the Euclidean metric on it\n([[lem-metrics-on-rn]]). Then:\n\n1. **Closed boxes are compact.** For reals $a_k \\le b_k$ $(k < n)$ the box\n   $Q = \\{\\, x \\in \\mathbb{R}^n : a_k \\le x_k \\le b_k \\text{ for every } k < n \\,\\}$\n   is a compact subset of $(\\mathbb{R}^n, d_2)$ ([[def-metric-compactness]]).\n2. **Heine-Borel.** A subset $K \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^n$ is a compact subset of\n   $(\\mathbb{R}^n, d_2)$ if and only if $K$ is closed in $\\mathbb{R}^n$\n   ([[def-metric-topology]]) and bounded\n   ([[def-metric-bounded-diameter]]).\n3. **The real line.** A subset $K \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ is a compact subset of\n   $(\\mathbb{R}, d_{\\mathbb{R}})$, the usual metric $d_{\\mathbb{R}}(x,y) = |x-y|$\n   ([[lem-real-line-is-a-metric-space]]), if and only if $K$ is closed in\n   $\\mathbb{R}$ and bounded.\n\n**No choice principle is used.** The bisection below halves one coordinate at a\ntime and takes the **left** half whenever the left half still fails to be finitely\ncovered, the right half otherwise: a rule with two outcomes, decided by a\nproperty of the box, not a selection. That is the whole reason the theorem is\navailable in ZF, while the general \"complete and totally bounded implies compact\"\n([[thm-complete-and-totally-bounded-implies-compact]]) is not.\n\nThe hypothesis $n \\ge 1$ is inherited from [[lem-metrics-on-rn]], which defines\n$\\mathbb{R}^n$ and its metrics only there; the last remark below records what\nhappens at $n = 0$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-tdmath-sector-nesting",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Under [[def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses]], the sectors are nested: if $y\\in A_x$, then $A_y\\subseteq A_x$; equality holds when $y\\in\\partial A_x$, and $A_y\\subset\\operatorname{int}_{\\Gamma_i}A_x$ when $y\\in\\operatorname{int}_{\\Gamma_i}A_x$. For every $x,y\\in\\Gamma_i$, either $x\\prec y$ or $y\\prec x$ (possibly both). Strict comparison is exactly interior membership: $x\\prec\\prec y$ if and only if $y\\in\\operatorname{int}_{\\Gamma_i}A_x$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "lem-tdmath-contact-energy-orientation",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let a family of simple domains satisfy [[def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses]], and suppose every pair has an admitted simple thermal join satisfying T1 and T2 of [[def-tdmath-thermal-contact-hypotheses]]. Then the fixed-work orientation signs from [[thm-tdmath-forward-boundary-graph]] agree throughout the family. One common affine proof-coordinate convention $u'=\\varepsilon u$, $\\varepsilon\\in\\{-1,1\\}$, therefore makes each fixed-work accessibility order increase with $u'$ and preserves the energy-sum formula for joins. This is a mathematical coordinate convention; it does not change a physical energy quantity or assert a sign of a measured temperature.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Use [F7]'s common mathematical energy orientation for the proof coordinates. Apply [F2] using [F1] after dividing represented coefficient maps by the fixed positive type weight $w_\\Gamma$, as specified in [A1]. Multiply the resulting unit-state entropy by $w_\\Gamma$ to evaluate represented states; this positive factor preserves all same-type order comparisons. Write $S(x)$ for that represented-state value. The convex-combination access hypothesis in [A1] and additivity imply concavity: for $0<t<1$, accessibility of $(tx,(1-t)y)$ to $tx+(1-t)y$ gives $tS(x)+(1-t)S(y)\\le S(tx+(1-t)y)$. By [F3], $S$ is strictly increasing in energy at fixed work coordinate.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F7"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "Fix a work coordinate $v$ and let $J_v:=\\{u:(u,v)\\in\\Gamma\\}$, an open interval. The restriction $s_v(u)=S(u,v)$ is finite, concave, continuous by [F4], and strictly increasing by [F3]. For an interior $u$, concavity makes the right secant slopes nonincreasing as the secant endpoint moves right, and the left secant slopes monotone as the endpoint approaches $u$; hence the one-sided derivative limits $s'_{v,+}(u)$ and $s'_{v,-}(u)$ exist. Choose $a<u<b$ in $J_v$: the secants to $a,b$ bound those limits, so they are finite, and concavity gives $s'_-\\ge s'_+$. Monotonicity gives nonnegative slopes. If $s'_+(u)=0$, then for every $z>u$ concavity bounds $(s_v(z)-s_v(u))/(z-u)\\le s'_+(u)=0$, contradicting strict increase. Thus $s'_-\\ge s'_+>0$. Define $\\Theta(u,v)=[1/s'_{v,-}(u),1/s'_{v,+}(u)]$, a nonempty compact interval of positive finite reciprocal slopes.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-3",
          "claim": "Suppose the second alternative fails, so each adiabat contains one thermal-equivalence class. By [F1]–[F2], $A_x=\\{y:S(y)\\ge S(x)\\}$. Continuity of $S$ and strict increase in energy at fixed work coordinate imply that every point with $S=\\sigma$ has arbitrarily nearby points with larger and smaller entropy; hence each entropy level $E_\\sigma=\\{x:S(x)=\\sigma\\}$ is a forward-sector boundary. The supposition makes each $E_\\sigma$ lie in one thermal class. Let $R=S(\\Gamma)$ and, for each thermal class $C_\\alpha$, put $R_\\alpha=\\{S(x):x\\in C_\\alpha\\}$. These sets partition $R$: two distinct classes cannot share a level under the supposition. The range $R$ is an interval, since any two points of the convex domain are joined by a segment and the continuous entropy takes every intermediate value along it.",
          "step": "1.3",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "A2",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Fix $x=(u_1,v_1)$ and $y=(u_2,v_2)$ of this same simple type, and let $U=u_1+u_2$. The admissible energy splits form the open interval $D=\\{w:(U-w,v_1)\\in\\Gamma,\\ (w,v_2)\\in\\Gamma\\}$, which contains $u_2$. Define $g(w)=s_{v_1}(U-w)+s_{v_2}(w)$. For the join state $J(x,y)$, T2 gives an admissible split $q$ whose pair is reversible with the join. For every other split $p$, T1 gives $p\\prec J(x,y)\\prec q$, so $p\\prec q$. The pair states $p,q$ are finite compounds of two copies of this same simple type; [F1]–[F2] and their entropy representation therefore give $g(p)\\le g(q)$. Thus $q$ maximizes $g$. If the initial split $x,y$ is reversible with the join, it is equivalent to $q$; entropy representation gives equal entropy, so it too maximizes $g$. Conversely, if $u_2$ maximizes $g$, then $g(u_2)=g(q)$, and [F1]–[F2] make the initial pair reversible with $q$; composing with $q\\sim J(x,y)$ shows $x\\sim_T y$. For a concave function at an interior point, maximality is equivalent to $g'_+(u_2)\\le0\\le g'_-(u_2)$: right secants are bounded above by the right derivative and left secants below by the left derivative. Here\n$g'_+(u_2)=-s'_{v_1,-}(u_1)+s'_{v_2,+}(u_2),\\qquad\ng'_-(u_2)=-s'_{v_1,+}(u_1)+s'_{v_2,-}(u_2).$\nThe two inequalities are exactly $s'_{v_1,+}(u_1)\\le s'_{v_2,-}(u_2)$ and $s'_{v_2,+}(u_2)\\le s'_{v_1,-}(u_1)$, which say the positive slope intervals intersect; reciprocal reverses their endpoints, so this is equivalent to $\\Theta(x)\\cap\\Theta(y)\\ne\\varnothing$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "A2",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "step 1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Let $T_\\alpha$ be one thermal class. The compact intervals $\\Theta(x)$, $x\\in T_\\alpha$, pairwise intersect by step 2.1. For any finite subfamily, pairwise intersection implies common intersection: the largest left endpoint is no greater than the smallest right endpoint, since the intervals attaining those two endpoints intersect. Fix $x_0\\in T_\\alpha$; the sets $\\Theta(x)\\cap\\Theta(x_0)$ are closed subsets of the compact interval $\\Theta(x_0)$ and have the finite-intersection property. Compactness [F5] gives a common point, so $I_\\alpha:=\\bigcap_{x\\in T_\\alpha}\\Theta(x)$ is a nonempty compact interval. If distinct classes had intersecting common intervals, representatives $x,y$ would have intersecting $\\Theta$ intervals and would be thermally equivalent by step 2.1, a contradiction. Thus the intervals $I_\\alpha$ for distinct classes are disjoint and strictly ordered on the real line; use this as the order of the classes.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A2",
            "F5",
            "step 2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "For each work coordinate $v$, strict continuity of $s_v$ maps the open interval $J_v$ onto an open interval $R_v\\subset R$. On $R_v$, the level-to-class map is nondecreasing: as $u$ increases, concavity makes both one-sided slopes nonincreasing, so both endpoints of $\\Theta(u,v)$ are nondecreasing. If two such intervals $\\Theta$ are disjoint, monotonicity of both endpoints forces the earlier interval to lie strictly below the later one; by step 3.1, this is the class order. The local class maps agree on overlaps of $R_v$ because each entropy level lies in one class. The open intervals $R_v$ cover $R$. Any compact subinterval of $R$ has a finite subcover by these intervals by [F5]; its intersection graph is connected, since otherwise the connected subinterval would be split into two disjoint nonempty relatively open sets. Chaining the local orders through overlaps proves that the class map is nondecreasing on all of $R$.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "step 1.2",
            "step 1.3",
            "step 3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-5-1",
          "claim": "Fix $\\sigma\\in R_\\alpha$ and $x\\in E_\\sigma$. T4 gives $x_0\\prec\\prec x\\prec\\prec x_1$ with $x_0\\sim_T x_1$. Entropy representation gives $S(x_0)<\\sigma<S(x_1)$, and their levels belong to the same class $R_\\alpha$. The global nondecreasing order from step 4.1 forces every level between them, including an open neighborhood of $\\sigma$, into $R_\\alpha$. Thus each class set is open in the interval $R$. Since these disjoint open sets partition connected $R$, only one nonempty class exists. Under the supposition, all states are thermally equivalent, proving the dichotomy.",
          "step": "5.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A2",
            "F2",
            "step 4.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
