        "status": "complete",
        "reviewer": "alpha-batch-6",
        "notes": "All nine steps checked against T1–T5 and explicit C1. Opposed component increments in two reversible splits give a calibrator. Assuming none, cancellation forces all splits on a join adiabat onto fixed component adiabats, and coordinate-axis paths in its connected work projection propagate one thermal class using C1. The dichotomy then makes each join one thermal class; T5 and C1 propagate to components. A small energy redistribution contradicts uniqueness of the split. Signed reductions identify reference increments, including negative coordinate coefficients; fixed-composition base terms cancel and meter increments compare both ways. Zero mass yields empty compounds. The common energy sign is mathematical only and empirical claims are absent."
      }
    },
    "thm-tdmath-arbitrary-family-calibration": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> ([[def-choice-function]]).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-tdmath-conditional-contact-calibrator",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $\\Gamma_1,\\Gamma_2$ be simple domains in a family of finite thermal joins satisfying A1–A7, S1–S3, T1–T5, and C1 of [[def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses]] and [[def-tdmath-thermal-contact-hypotheses]]. Then there are $x_0\\prec\\prec x_1$ in $\\Gamma_1$ and $y_0\\prec\\prec y_1$ in $\\Gamma_2$ such that\n\n$$(x_0,y_1)\\sim(x_1,y_0).$$\n\nMoreover, for every fixed finite composition of scaled copies of $\\Gamma_1$ and $\\Gamma_2$, any two equal-mass states of that same composition are comparable (CH). The conclusion is conditional on the explicitly stated join-coherence and universal-range assumptions C1 and T5.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-tdmath-additive-entropy-representation",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $X$ be nonempty and suppose its finite-support compounds carry the comparison structure of [[def-tdmath-comparison-and-stability]] over the complete ordered field $\\mathbb R$. If there are $x_0,x_1\\in X$ with $x_0\\preceq x_1$ and not $x_1\\preceq x_0$, define $S(x):=\\sup L_x$ using [[lem-tdmath-reference-supremum-bounds]]. Then $S:X\\to\\mathbb R$ is normalized by $S(x_0)=0$, $S(x_1)=1$, and for all equal-mass compounds $A=\\sum_i a_i e_{x_i}$ and $B=\\sum_j b_j e_{y_j}$,\n\n$$A\\preceq B\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\\sum_i a_iS(x_i)\\le\\sum_j b_jS(y_j).$$\n\nThus $S$ is an additive extensive representation on finite compounds. If no strict pair exists, CH instead makes every equal-mass pair of compounds equivalent, and any constant state function gives a (degenerate) additive representation. No concavity, continuity, or physical law follows without additional hypotheses.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-tdmath-forward-signed-comparison",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $u,v$ be finite-support real coefficient maps on the tagged state labels of [[def-tdmath-tagged-scalable-compound]] with equal total coefficient. For a typed state $x$, compound notation uses the represented map $\\mathsf{J}(x)$ from [[def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses]]; its raw unit point mass is $e_{(\\Gamma,x)}$. Signed maps still use the ordinary total of their raw coefficients, exactly the bookkeeping mass of the represented nonnegative maps. Put $d=v-u$, $d^+(x)=\\max(d(x),0)$ and $d^-(x)=\\max(-d(x),0)$. Define $u\\preceq_s v$ to mean $d^-\\prec d^+$ under the compound relation of [[def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses]]. Write $u\\sim_s v$ when both $u\\preceq_s v$ and $v\\preceq_s u$. These are formal signed comparison maps, not physical negative amounts. The positive and negative parts are finite-support compounds of equal mass. If $u\\sim_s v$, then $cu\\sim_s cv$ for every real $c$: positive $c$ uses scaling, $c=0$ is reflexivity, and negative $c$ swaps the positive and negative parts while equivalence supplies both comparison directions. This Definition introduces the signed notation; ordinary-order compatibility and the signed-order laws are proved separately.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-tdmath-forward-spectator-cancellation",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Under A1–A6 of [[def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses]], for compounds $A,B$ with $m(A)=m(B)$ and every finite spectator compound $C$, $A+C\\prec B+C$ if and only if $A\\prec B$. Consequently, for finite-support signed maps $u,v$ of equal total coefficient, define $u\\preceq_s v$ by $(v-u)^-\\prec(v-u)^+$. This relation is well-defined, agrees with $\\prec$ on nonnegative maps, is invariant under adding a common signed map, is transitive, and is closed under addition of two comparisons. No comparison hypothesis is used.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Use the positive type normalization in [[def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses]] whenever invoking [F2]; state entropies evaluate represented maps $\\mathsf{J}(x)$, and their unit-label entropy density is $S_i(x)/w_{\\Gamma_i}$. Apply [F2] to the meter type and choose its entropy $S_m$ so $S_m(z_0)=0$ and $S_m(z_1)=1$. Set the meter reference to $r_m=z_0$, and use [A1] to choose a reference state $r_i$ and one same-type entropy representative for every $i\\ne m$. For each $i\\ne m$, use [F1] to choose calibrator states $a_i,b_i\\in\\Gamma_i$ and $c_i,d_i\\in\\Gamma_m$ such that $a_i\\prec\\prec b_i$, $c_i\\prec\\prec d_i$, and $(a_i,d_i)\\sim(b_i,c_i)$; also choose $r_i\\in\\Gamma_i$. For the meter label set $a_m=c_m=z_0$ and $b_m=d_m=z_1$; the two pairs $(z_0,z_1)$ and $(z_1,z_0)$ are the same two-copy meter compound up to permutation. Write $S_i^0$ for the selected entropy representative, with $S_m^0=S_m$, and put $\\Delta_i=S_i^0(b_i)-S_i^0(a_i)>0$ and $\\mu_i=S_m(d_i)-S_m(c_i)>0$. Define $S_i(x):=\\frac{\\mu_i}{\\Delta_i}\\bigl(S_i^0(x)-S_i^0(r_i)\\bigr)$. Then $S_i(r_i)=0$ and $S_i(b_i)-S_i(a_i)=\\mu_i$, matching the meter entropy increment across the chosen calibrator endpoints. For $i=m$, $\\Delta_m=\\mu_m=1$, so this agrees with the fixed meter normalization.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "choose"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "For a finite separated compound $A$ with factors $(t_\\ell i_\\ell,x_\\ell)$, define $S(A):=\\sum_\\ell t_\\ell S_{i_\\ell}(x_\\ell)$. The sum is finite and invariant under reordering; concatenating compounds adds the sums, and scaling every amount by $t>0$ multiplies it by $t$. The explicit-factor convention in the Statement therefore makes $S$ well-defined, additive, and extensive.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "step 1.1",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Fix a composition with amounts $M_i$ for finitely many labels and let $A,B$ be two states of that same composition. For a state $x$ of type $i$, put\n$s_i(x):=\\frac{S_i(x)-S_i(a_i)}{\\mu_i}.$\nThe same-type entropy representation and the CH for type $i$ show\n$\\mathsf{J}(x)\\sim_s(1-s_i(x))\\mathsf{J}(a_i)+s_i(x)\\mathsf{J}(b_i).$\nIndeed, the signed difference has total coefficient zero and $S_i$-value zero, so its positive and negative parts have equal mass and entropy and therefore compare both ways by [F2]. The calibrator relation gives\n$(\\mathsf{J}(b_i)-\\mathsf{J}(a_i))\\sim_s(\\mathsf{J}(d_i)-\\mathsf{J}(c_i))$\nby taking the signed difference of $(a_i,d_i)\\sim(b_i,c_i)$ and canceling common terms with [F3]. Add and scale these identities over the finitely many factors of $A$. With\n$q_i(A):=\\sum_{\\ell:\\,i_\\ell=i}t_\\ell s_i(x_\\ell),$\nthe resulting signed map is\n$A\\sim_s \\sum_i M_i \\mathsf{J}(a_i)+\\sum_i q_i(A)(\\mathsf{J}(d_i)-\\mathsf{J}(c_i)),$\nwhere the sums range over the finite support of the composition. The same holds for $B$. Since they have the same composition, the reference base cancels, and $B-A$ is signed-equivalent to the finite meter map\n$D:=\\sum_i\\bigl(q_i(B)-q_i(A)\\bigr)(\\mathsf{J}(d_i)-\\mathsf{J}(c_i)).$\nExtend the meter entropy linearly to raw finite signed coefficient maps using density $S_m(z)/w_{\\Gamma_m}$ at a meter raw tag $(\\Gamma_m,z)$; in particular its value on $\\mathsf{J}(z)$ is $S_m(z)$. Then\n$S_m(D)=\\sum_i\\bigl(q_i(B)-q_i(A)\\bigr)\\mu_i=S(B)-S(A).$\nThe positive and negative parts of $D$ have equal mass. If $S(A)\\le S(B)$, the same-type entropy representation [F2] for finite meter products gives $D^-\\prec D^+$, hence $D$ compares in the forward signed direction. Translation, addition, and ordinary-order compatibility [F3] transfer this to $A\\prec B$. Conversely, if $A\\prec B$, the same signed equivalences and translation in [F3] give $D^-\\prec D^+$. The meter representation in [F2] then forces $S_m(D)=S(B)-S(A)\\ge0$. Reversing the entropy inequality gives the reverse comparison. Thus $S$ characterizes every same-composition comparison. Each particular comparison uses only finitely many labels; no infinite sum or uncountable product occurs.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "The meter normalization fixes the multiplicative unit. Changing the chosen reference state for type $i$ changes only its additive constant; on a finite compound these constants contribute amount-weighted conserved-type offsets. This proves the stated freedom and completes the construction.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement primitive index I is nonempty and contains the distinguished meter. A finite product may have no factors; its represented map and finite entropy sum are then both zero and comparison is reflexive.",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Statement",
            "source_excerpt": "Let $I$ be a nonempty set of primitive simple-system labels containing a distinguished meter label $m$. Assume every finite separated product is explicitly represented by its finitely many tagged elementary factors; positive scaling and finite product are the only identifications. Suppose the join-closed family satisfies the hypotheses of [[thm-tdmath-conditional-contact-calibrator]], and fix a strict meter pair $z_0\\prec\\prec z_1$ in $\\Gamma_m$. Assume the Axiom of Choice. Then one can choose, for every $i\\in I$, an additive constant and positive scale for its same-type entropy so that\n\n$$S\\big((t_1i_1,x_1),\\ldots,(t_ni_n,x_n)\\big)=\\sum_{\\ell=1}^n t_\\ell S_{i_\\ell}(x_\\ell)$$\n\nis additive, extensive, and characterizes every same-composition comparison on every finite separated product. No conclusion is asserted for transitions that change composition.",
            "current_raw_sha256": "4d0717659d2951dc0c43200b616e4d72f4b03b072cd1c42027abe19346d31ac4",
