    "def-tdmath-decorated-chain-costs": {
      "citations": [],
      "derivations": [],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Definition the empty unit has X_1={*}, zero mass and zero entropy. A decorated chain still has N>=1 actual edges; absence of a chain yields F=+infinity, rather than an invented witness.",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Definition",
            "source_excerpt": "Let $\\mathcal G$ be a set of tagged system types closed under finite composition $T\\times T'$ and positive scaling $tT$. Scaling is associative and acts componentwise on products: $s(tT)=(st)T$ and $t(T\\times T')=(tT)\\times(tT')$. Each type $T$ has a nonempty state set $X_T$ of finite tagged compounds, every state in $X_T$ is represented by the coefficient-map interpretation of [[def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses]] and has the fixed aggregate composition $\\nu(a)=\\nu_T$ of its declared type, using the amount-function interpretation in [[def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses]], and has total coefficient mass $m(T)=w_T\\ge0$, and $\\nu_{T\\times T'}=\\nu_T+\\nu_{T'}$, $\\nu_{tT}=t\\nu_T$, with $m(T\\times T')=m(T)+m(T')$ and $m(tT)=t\\,m(T)$. Matching scalar masses alone do not identify aggregate composition. The entropy $\\sigma_T:X_T\\to\\mathbb R$ is the finite additive extensive entropy from [[thm-tdmath-arbitrary-family-calibration]]; in particular $\\sigma_{tT}(ta)=t\\sigma_T(a)$.\n\nFor each pair of types prescribe the product map $\\pi_{T,T\\prime}:X_T\\times X_{T\\prime}\\to X_{T\\times T\\prime}$ by $\\pi(a,b)=a+b$ on represented coefficient maps, and require it to be surjective: $X_{T\\times T\\prime}=\\{a+b:a\\in X_T,b\\in X_{T\\prime}\\}$. Thus each product state admits a factorization, without requiring it to be unique. For $t>0$, prescribe the scaling bijection $s_{t,T}:X_T\\to X_{tT}$ by $s_{t,T}(a)=ta$, with inverse coefficient scaling by $1/t$. These maps commute with the stated type identifications. Require $\\sigma_{T\\times T\\prime}(a+b)=\\sigma_T(a)+\\sigma_{T\\prime}(b)$ and $\\sigma_{tT}(ta)=t\\sigma_T(a)$; entropy is therefore independent of any nonunique product factorization. Include the formal empty unit type $1$, with $X_1=\\{0\\}$, where $0$ is the empty coefficient map, $m(1)=0$, $\\sigma_1(0)=0$, $T\\times1=T$, and $t1=1$. A transition edge from type $T$ to type $T'$ is a pair $a\\in X_T$, $b\\in X_{T'}$ such that the underlying equal-mass compounds satisfy $a\\prec b$ under [[def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses]]. Thus an edge can exist only when $m(T)=m(T')$. Its cost is the finite real number $\\sigma_{T'}(b)-\\sigma_T(a)$.\n\nA decorated finite chain from $\\Gamma$ to $\\Gamma'$ consists of a catalyst type $C\\in\\mathcal G$, an integer $N\\ge1$, types $T_0=\\Gamma\\times C,T_1,\\ldots,T_N=\\Gamma'\\times C$, and states $a_k\\in X_{T_k}$, $b_{k+1}\\in X_{T_{k+1}}$ with an edge $a_k\\prec b_{k+1}$ for every $0\\le k<N$. Its cost is the finite real sum\n\n$$c=\\sum_{k=0}^{N-1}\\bigl(\\sigma_{T_{k+1}}(b_{k+1})-\\sigma_{T_k}(a_k)\\bigr).$$\n\nDefine the ordered extended real line $\\overline{\\mathbb R}=\\mathbb R\\cup\\{-\\infty,+\\infty\\}$ by placing $-\\infty$ below every real and $+\\infty$ above every real. Finite addition retains its usual meaning; a real added to either infinity leaves that infinity, and equal-sign infinities add to themselves. Opposite-sign infinite sums are undefined. Positive multiplication preserves each infinity, and negation exchanges the infinities. For a nonempty real set bounded below, its infimum is its greatest real lower bound; completeness ensures existence. Define $F:\\mathcal G\\times\\mathcal G\\to\\overline{\\mathbb R}$ by taking $F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma\\prime)$ as the infimum of all such witness costs. Set $F=+\\infty$ if there is no witness and $F=-\\infty$ if the witness costs are unbounded below. The infimum is over real witness costs, so no subtraction of extended-real infima is used. Type permutations, reassociation, reversible identifications, and the identity $T\\times1=T$ are part of the declared type structure. The cost identities and inequalities require a separate proof.",
            "current_raw_sha256": "134a3faa318ec50105651fabf648a716f800b901947b77799d5cc77f1acb31da",
            "scope_only_not_proof_acceptance": true
          }
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Definition",
            "source_excerpt": "Let $\\mathcal G$ be a set of tagged system types closed under finite composition $T\\times T'$ and positive scaling $tT$. Scaling is associative and acts componentwise on products: $s(tT)=(st)T$ and $t(T\\times T')=(tT)\\times(tT')$. Each type $T$ has a nonempty state set $X_T$ of finite tagged compounds, every state in $X_T$ is represented by the coefficient-map interpretation of [[def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses]] and has the fixed aggregate composition $\\nu(a)=\\nu_T$ of its declared type, using the amount-function interpretation in [[def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses]], and has total coefficient mass $m(T)=w_T\\ge0$, and $\\nu_{T\\times T'}=\\nu_T+\\nu_{T'}$, $\\nu_{tT}=t\\nu_T$, with $m(T\\times T')=m(T)+m(T')$ and $m(tT)=t\\,m(T)$. Matching scalar masses alone do not identify aggregate composition. The entropy $\\sigma_T:X_T\\to\\mathbb R$ is the finite additive extensive entropy from [[thm-tdmath-arbitrary-family-calibration]]; in particular $\\sigma_{tT}(ta)=t\\sigma_T(a)$.\n\nFor each pair of types prescribe the product map $\\pi_{T,T\\prime}:X_T\\times X_{T\\prime}\\to X_{T\\times T\\prime}$ by $\\pi(a,b)=a+b$ on represented coefficient maps, and require it to be surjective: $X_{T\\times T\\prime}=\\{a+b:a\\in X_T,b\\in X_{T\\prime}\\}$. Thus each product state admits a factorization, without requiring it to be unique. For $t>0$, prescribe the scaling bijection $s_{t,T}:X_T\\to X_{tT}$ by $s_{t,T}(a)=ta$, with inverse coefficient scaling by $1/t$. These maps commute with the stated type identifications. Require $\\sigma_{T\\times T\\prime}(a+b)=\\sigma_T(a)+\\sigma_{T\\prime}(b)$ and $\\sigma_{tT}(ta)=t\\sigma_T(a)$; entropy is therefore independent of any nonunique product factorization. Include the formal empty unit type $1$, with $X_1=\\{0\\}$, where $0$ is the empty coefficient map, $m(1)=0$, $\\sigma_1(0)=0$, $T\\times1=T$, and $t1=1$. A transition edge from type $T$ to type $T'$ is a pair $a\\in X_T$, $b\\in X_{T'}$ such that the underlying equal-mass compounds satisfy $a\\prec b$ under [[def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses]]. Thus an edge can exist only when $m(T)=m(T')$. Its cost is the finite real number $\\sigma_{T'}(b)-\\sigma_T(a)$.\n\nA decorated finite chain from $\\Gamma$ to $\\Gamma'$ consists of a catalyst type $C\\in\\mathcal G$, an integer $N\\ge1$, types $T_0=\\Gamma\\times C,T_1,\\ldots,T_N=\\Gamma'\\times C$, and states $a_k\\in X_{T_k}$, $b_{k+1}\\in X_{T_{k+1}}$ with an edge $a_k\\prec b_{k+1}$ for every $0\\le k<N$. Its cost is the finite real sum\n\n$$c=\\sum_{k=0}^{N-1}\\bigl(\\sigma_{T_{k+1}}(b_{k+1})-\\sigma_{T_k}(a_k)\\bigr).$$\n\nDefine the ordered extended real line $\\overline{\\mathbb R}=\\mathbb R\\cup\\{-\\infty,+\\infty\\}$ by placing $-\\infty$ below every real and $+\\infty$ above every real. Finite addition retains its usual meaning; a real added to either infinity leaves that infinity, and equal-sign infinities add to themselves. Opposite-sign infinite sums are undefined. Positive multiplication preserves each infinity, and negation exchanges the infinities. For a nonempty real set bounded below, its infimum is its greatest real lower bound; completeness ensures existence. Define $F:\\mathcal G\\times\\mathcal G\\to\\overline{\\mathbb R}$ by taking $F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma\\prime)$ as the infimum of all such witness costs. Set $F=+\\infty$ if there is no witness and $F=-\\infty$ if the witness costs are unbounded below. The infimum is over real witness costs, so no subtraction of extended-real infima is used. Type permutations, reassociation, reversible identifications, and the identity $T\\times1=T$ are part of the declared type structure. The cost identities and inequalities require a separate proof.",
            "current_raw_sha256": "134a3faa318ec50105651fabf648a716f800b901947b77799d5cc77f1acb31da",
            "scope_only_not_proof_acceptance": true
          },
          "reason": "This is a Definition interface, not a theorem proof case. Its declared fixed aggregate composition for each X_T, finite real edge costs, empty-unit convention and empty-infimum/infinite-infimum rules are retained; this row asserts no new proof discharge or physical adoption."
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Definition",
            "source_excerpt": "Let $\\mathcal G$ be a set of tagged system types closed under finite composition $T\\times T'$ and positive scaling $tT$. Scaling is associative and acts componentwise on products: $s(tT)=(st)T$ and $t(T\\times T')=(tT)\\times(tT')$. Each type $T$ has a nonempty state set $X_T$ of finite tagged compounds, every state in $X_T$ is represented by the coefficient-map interpretation of [[def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses]] and has the fixed aggregate composition $\\nu(a)=\\nu_T$ of its declared type, using the amount-function interpretation in [[def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses]], and has total coefficient mass $m(T)=w_T\\ge0$, and $\\nu_{T\\times T'}=\\nu_T+\\nu_{T'}$, $\\nu_{tT}=t\\nu_T$, with $m(T\\times T')=m(T)+m(T')$ and $m(tT)=t\\,m(T)$. Matching scalar masses alone do not identify aggregate composition. The entropy $\\sigma_T:X_T\\to\\mathbb R$ is the finite additive extensive entropy from [[thm-tdmath-arbitrary-family-calibration]]; in particular $\\sigma_{tT}(ta)=t\\sigma_T(a)$.\n\nFor each pair of types prescribe the product map $\\pi_{T,T\\prime}:X_T\\times X_{T\\prime}\\to X_{T\\times T\\prime}$ by $\\pi(a,b)=a+b$ on represented coefficient maps, and require it to be surjective: $X_{T\\times T\\prime}=\\{a+b:a\\in X_T,b\\in X_{T\\prime}\\}$. Thus each product state admits a factorization, without requiring it to be unique. For $t>0$, prescribe the scaling bijection $s_{t,T}:X_T\\to X_{tT}$ by $s_{t,T}(a)=ta$, with inverse coefficient scaling by $1/t$. These maps commute with the stated type identifications. Require $\\sigma_{T\\times T\\prime}(a+b)=\\sigma_T(a)+\\sigma_{T\\prime}(b)$ and $\\sigma_{tT}(ta)=t\\sigma_T(a)$; entropy is therefore independent of any nonunique product factorization. Include the formal empty unit type $1$, with $X_1=\\{0\\}$, where $0$ is the empty coefficient map, $m(1)=0$, $\\sigma_1(0)=0$, $T\\times1=T$, and $t1=1$. A transition edge from type $T$ to type $T'$ is a pair $a\\in X_T$, $b\\in X_{T'}$ such that the underlying equal-mass compounds satisfy $a\\prec b$ under [[def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses]]. Thus an edge can exist only when $m(T)=m(T')$. Its cost is the finite real number $\\sigma_{T'}(b)-\\sigma_T(a)$.\n\nA decorated finite chain from $\\Gamma$ to $\\Gamma'$ consists of a catalyst type $C\\in\\mathcal G$, an integer $N\\ge1$, types $T_0=\\Gamma\\times C,T_1,\\ldots,T_N=\\Gamma'\\times C$, and states $a_k\\in X_{T_k}$, $b_{k+1}\\in X_{T_{k+1}}$ with an edge $a_k\\prec b_{k+1}$ for every $0\\le k<N$. Its cost is the finite real sum\n\n$$c=\\sum_{k=0}^{N-1}\\bigl(\\sigma_{T_{k+1}}(b_{k+1})-\\sigma_{T_k}(a_k)\\bigr).$$\n\nDefine the ordered extended real line $\\overline{\\mathbb R}=\\mathbb R\\cup\\{-\\infty,+\\infty\\}$ by placing $-\\infty$ below every real and $+\\infty$ above every real. Finite addition retains its usual meaning; a real added to either infinity leaves that infinity, and equal-sign infinities add to themselves. Opposite-sign infinite sums are undefined. Positive multiplication preserves each infinity, and negation exchanges the infinities. For a nonempty real set bounded below, its infimum is its greatest real lower bound; completeness ensures existence. Define $F:\\mathcal G\\times\\mathcal G\\to\\overline{\\mathbb R}$ by taking $F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma\\prime)$ as the infimum of all such witness costs. Set $F=+\\infty$ if there is no witness and $F=-\\infty$ if the witness costs are unbounded below. The infimum is over real witness costs, so no subtraction of extended-real infima is used. Type permutations, reassociation, reversible identifications, and the identity $T\\times1=T$ are part of the declared type structure. The cost identities and inequalities require a separate proof.",
            "current_raw_sha256": "134a3faa318ec50105651fabf648a716f800b901947b77799d5cc77f1acb31da",
            "scope_only_not_proof_acceptance": true
