_{T'}$ such that the underlying equal-mass compounds satisfy $a\\prec b$ under [[def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses]]. Thus an edge can exist only when $m(T)=m(T')$. Its cost is the finite real number $\\sigma_{T'}(b)-\\sigma_T(a)$.\n\nA decorated finite chain from $\\Gamma$ to $\\Gamma'$ consists of a catalyst type $C\\in\\mathcal G$, an integer $N\\ge1$, types $T_0=\\Gamma\\times C,T_1,\\ldots,T_N=\\Gamma'\\times C$, and states $a_k\\in X_{T_k}$, $b_{k+1}\\in X_{T_{k+1}}$ with an edge $a_k\\prec b_{k+1}$ for every $0\\le k<N$. Its cost is the finite real sum\n\n$$c=\\sum_{k=0}^{N-1}\\bigl(\\sigma_{T_{k+1}}(b_{k+1})-\\sigma_{T_k}(a_k)\\bigr).$$\n\nDefine the ordered extended real line $\\overline{\\mathbb R}=\\mathbb R\\cup\\{-\\infty,+\\infty\\}$ by placing $-\\infty$ below every real and $+\\infty$ above every real. Finite addition retains its usual meaning; a real added to either infinity leaves that infinity, and equal-sign infinities add to themselves. Opposite-sign infinite sums are undefined. Positive multiplication preserves each infinity, and negation exchanges the infinities. For a nonempty real set bounded below, its infimum is its greatest real lower bound; completeness ensures existence. Define $F:\\mathcal G\\times\\mathcal G\\to\\overline{\\mathbb R}$ by taking $F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma\\prime)$ as the infimum of all such witness costs. Set $F=+\\infty$ if there is no witness and $F=-\\infty$ if the witness costs are unbounded below. The infimum is over real witness costs, so no subtraction of extended-real infima is used. Type permutations, reassociation, reversible identifications, and the identity $T\\times1=T$ are part of the declared type structure. The cost identities and inequalities require a separate proof.",
