    "thm-tdmath-unattained-cost-entropy-inference": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $P$ be a set of primitive type labels. A formal type $\\Gamma$ records a finitely supported amount function $\\nu_\\Gamma:P\\to\\mathbb R_{\\ge0}$, not identically zero. Primitive generators have their unit amount function. Positive scaling, separated product, and thermal-join constructors retain distinct formal tags and obey\n\n$$\\nu_{s\\Gamma}=s\\nu_\\Gamma,\\qquad \\nu_{\\Gamma\\otimes\\Gamma'}=\\nu_\\Gamma+\\nu_{\\Gamma'}\\qquad(s>0).$$\n\nSeparated and joined types are distinct constructors even when their amount functions agree. Permutation, reassociation, and scaled-splitting conventions preserve the amount function. Define the positive **bookkeeping weight** $w_\\Gamma:=\\sum_{p\\in P}\\nu_\\Gamma(p)$, a finite sum over support. Thus $w_{s\\Gamma}=s w_\\Gamma$ and every product or join has weight $w_\\Gamma+w_{\\Gamma'}$. The optional empty unit has zero amount function and weight zero; it is represented only by the empty compound, not as a simple chart. These are formal amounts, not masses in kilograms or an empirical conservation law. The functions and pointwise operations use [[def-function-space]]; their finite-support convention and finite sums are those of [[def-tdmath-tagged-scalable-compound]] and [[lem-tdmath-compound-mass-well-defined]].\n\nFor Euclidean vectors use $a\\cdot b=\\sum_j a_jb_j$ and $\\|a\\|=(a\\cdot a)^{1/2}$. Write $B(a,r)=\\{b:\\|b-a\\|<r\\}$. Open means each point has such a ball contained in the set; convex means $(1-t)a+tb$ belongs to the set for all its $a,b$ and $0\\le t\\le1$. In a subspace or subset, use intersections of these balls for the relative topology. A map $P:D\\to\\mathbb R^n$ is locally Lipschitz if each $a\\in D$ has a relative neighborhood $V$ and finite $L\\ge0$ with $\\|P(b)-P(c)\\|\\le L\\|b-c\\|$ for all $b,c\\in V$. A set $D$ is arcwise connected here if each two distinct points are joined by a continuous injective map $\\gamma:[0,1]\\to D$ with those endpoints; singleton and empty sets satisfy this condition vacuously. Continuity uses the indicated Euclidean relative topology. Let $I$ index the declared family of simple types $\\Gamma_i$. For each simple type $\\Gamma$ in that family, let $n_\\Gamma\\ge1$ and let $\\Gamma\\subset\\mathbb R^{n_\\Gamma+1}$ be nonempty, open and convex, with coordinates $x=(u,v)$. The notation for a type and its chart is shared, but each state has a distinct raw tag $(\\Gamma,x)$. Scaled charts are distinct tagged copies $s\\Gamma=\\{sx:x\\in\\Gamma\\}$ with coordinate transport $x\\mapsto sx$; transported raw tags remain distinct from the original raw tags. Let $e_{(\\Gamma,x)}$ be the unit point mass from [[def-tdmath-tagged-scalable-compound]]. The represented state is\n\n$$\\mathsf{J}_\\Gamma(x):=w_\\Gamma e_{(\\Gamma,x)},\\qquad m(\\mathsf{J}_\\Gamma(x))=w_\\Gamma.$$\n\nWrite $\\mathsf{J}(x):=\\mathsf{J}_\\Gamma(x)$ when $x$ already carries its type tag. All state notation in this framework's compound comparisons, including a bare $x$, denotes $\\mathsf{J}_\\Gamma(x)$; a raw unit point mass is always written $e_{(\\Gamma,x)}$. A separated pair is addition of represented states, not a unit product tag. Nonnegative multiples are pointwise and zero terms are omitted. For an encoded compound $c$, its aggregate composition is the finite amount function\n\n$$\\nu(c):=\\sum_{(\\Gamma,x)\\in\\operatorname{supp}(c)}\\frac{c(\\Gamma,x)}{w_\\Gamma}\\nu_\\Gamma;\\qquad m(c)=\\sum_{p\\in P}\\nu(c)(p).$$\n\nFix the formal interpretation $\\Pi(c)$ as the finite separated preparation whose factor at each nonzero raw tag $(\\Gamma,x)$ is the amount $c(\\Gamma,x)/w_\\Gamma$ of that typed state, using the declared coordinate scaling transport. The accessibility relation on coefficient maps is the pullback of the declared relation on these preparations. Merging or splitting repeated copies of the same preparation is reversible; permutation and reassociation preserve the preparation. Thus $\\Pi(\\mathsf{J}_{s\\Gamma}(sx))$ and $\\Pi(s\\mathsf{J}_\\Gamma(x))$ are the same declared scaled preparation and yield\n\n$$\\mathsf{J}_{s\\Gamma}(sx)\\sim s\\mathsf{J}_\\Gamma(x),$$\n\nalthough their raw point masses have different tags and are not equal. This reversible transport commutes with finite composition, nested type constructors, and scaled splitting. It is part of the precise formal scaling interpretation, not a deduction about physical accessibility from coordinate multiplication. A physical use must adopt the stated preparation maps and accessibility assumptions; fixed-composition models compare only preparations with the same $\\nu$, while a declared channel model may additionally compare different compositions of equal $m$.\n\nA comparison $A\\prec B$ is defined only when $m(A)=m(B)$, as in the coefficient-map supplier. Write $A\\sim B$ when both directions hold, and $A\\prec\\prec B$ when only the forward direction holds. On any fixed simple type, dividing every represented state by the same positive constant $w_\\Gamma$ gives its unit-point-mass comparison. Positive scaling and its inverse preserve and reflect order; whenever the required CH and reference hypotheses hold, the unit-state entropy theorem applies after that normalization. If $S$ represents unit-point-mass comparisons, the entropy of a represented state is $w_\\Gamma S(x)$ and the entropy of a finite sum $\\sum_j t_j\\mathsf{J}_\\Gamma(x_j)$ is $\\sum_j t_j w_\\Gamma S(x_j)$. No independent rescaling across different types is assumed without a calibrator.\n\nThe common relation satisfies A1–A6 on all equal-mass finite compounds: (A1) reflexivity; (A2) transitivity; (A3) if $A\\prec B$, then $A+C\\prec B+C$ for every compound $C$; (A4) if $A\\prec B$, then $tA\\prec tB$ for every $t>0$; (A5) $t\\mathsf{J}_\\Gamma(x)$ is equivalent to $s\\mathsf{J}_\\Gamma(x)+(t-s)\\mathsf{J}_\\Gamma(x)$ for $t>0$ and $0<s<t$, with the reversible scaled-copy transport above; (A6) for equal-mass $A,B$, fixed positive equal-mass spectators $Z_0,Z_1$, and every sequence $\\epsilon_n>0$ decreasing to zero, $A+\\epsilon_n Z_0\\prec B+\\epsilon_n Z_1$ for all $n$ implies $A\\prec B$. These are hypotheses, not conclusions. No comparison hypothesis is included here.\n\nFor each $i$ and $x\\in\\Gamma_i$, define the forward sector $A_x:=\\{y\\in\\Gamma_i:\\mathsf{J}_{\\Gamma_i}(x)\\prec\\mathsf{J}_{\\Gamma_i}(y)\\}$. The simple-domain assumptions are: (A7) for $x,y\\in\\Gamma_i$ and $0<t<1$, the encoded compound $t\\mathsf{J}_{\\Gamma_i}(x)+(1-t)\\mathsf{J}_{\\Gamma_i}(y)$ compares to $\\mathsf{J}_{\\Gamma_i}(tx+(1-t)y)$; both have bookkeeping mass $w_{\\Gamma_i}$, and convexity puts the recombined coordinate in the chart; (S1) each $x$ has a strict successor; (S2) $A_x$ has exactly one supporting hyperplane at $x$, expressible as $u-u_x+P_i(x)\\cdot(v-v_x)=0$ with a finite-valued slope map $P_i:\\Gamma_i\\to\\mathbb R^{n_i}$, and $P_i$ is locally Lipschitz; (S3) the boundary of $A_x$ relative to $\\Gamma_i$ is arcwise connected. A supporting hyperplane means that $A_x$ lies in one of its closed half-spaces and meets the plane at $x$. The affine hull is the smallest affine subspace containing a set; relative interior is interior in that affine hull. Closure and boundary are taken in the indicated affine hull or in $\\Gamma_i$, as named; boundary is closure intersected with closure of the complement in that relative topology.\n\nThis definition imposes no physical interpretation, units, or empirical claim. A contact theorem that uses joins must separately assume that each join is a simple domain satisfying these same forward-sector hypotheses.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-tdmath-arbitrary-family-calibration",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $I$ be a nonempty set of primitive simple-system labels containing a distinguished meter label $m$. Assume every finite separated product is explicitly represented by its finitely many tagged elementary factors; positive scaling and finite product are the only identifications. Suppose the join-closed family satisfies the hypotheses of [[thm-tdmath-conditional-contact-calibrator]], and fix a strict meter pair $z_0\\prec\\prec z_1$ in $\\Gamma_m$. Assume the Axiom of Choice. Then one can choose, for every $i\\in I$, an additive constant and positive scale for its same-type entropy so that\n\n$$S\\big((t_1i_1,x_1),\\ldots,(t_ni_n,x_n)\\big)=\\sum_{\\ell=1}^n t_\\ell S_{i_\\ell}(x_\\ell)$$\n\nis additive, extensive, and characterizes every same-composition comparison on every finite separated product. No conclusion is asserted for transitions that change composition.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-tdmath-decorated-chain-costs",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $\\mathcal G$ be a set of tagged system types closed under finite composition $T\\times T'$ and positive scaling $tT$. Scaling is associative and acts componentwise on products: $s(tT)=(st)T$ and $t(T\\times T')=(tT)\\times(tT')$. Each type $T$ has a nonempty state set $X_T$ of finite tagged compounds, every state in $X_T$ is represented by the coefficient-map interpretation of [[def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses]] and has the fixed aggregate composition $\\nu(a)=\\nu_T$ of its declared type, using the amount-function interpretation in [[def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses]], and has total coefficient mass $m(T)=w_T\\ge0$, and $\\nu_{T\\times T'}=\\nu_T+\\nu_{T'}$, $\\nu_{tT}=t\\nu_T$, with $m(T\\times T')=m(T)+m(T')$ and $m(tT)=t\\,m(T)$. Matching scalar masses alone do not identify aggregate composition. The entropy $\\sigma_T:X_T\\to\\mathbb R$ is the finite additive extensive entropy from [[thm-tdmath-arbitrary-family-calibration]]; in particular $\\sigma_{tT}(ta)=t\\sigma_T(a)$.\n\nFor each pair of types prescribe the product map $\\pi_{T,T\\prime}:X_T\\times X_{T\\prime}\\to X_{T\\times T\\prime}$ by $\\pi(a,b)=a+b$ on represented coefficient maps, and require it to be surjective: $X_{T\\times T\\prime}=\\{a+b:a\\in X_T,b\\in X_{T\\prime}\\}$. Thus each product state admits a factorization, without requiring it to be unique. For $t>0$, prescribe the scaling bijection $s_{t,T}:X_T\\to X_{tT}$ by $s_{t,T}(a)=ta$, with inverse coefficient scaling by $1/t$. These maps commute with the stated type identifications. Require $\\sigma_{T\\times T\\prime}(a+b)=\\sigma_T(a)+\\sigma_{T\\prime}(b)$ and $\\sigma_{tT}(ta)=t\\sigma_T(a)$; entropy is therefore independent of any nonunique product factorization. Include the formal empty unit type $1$, with $X_1=\\{0\\}$, where $0$ is the empty coefficient map, $m(1)=0$, $\\sigma_1(0)=0$, $T\\times1=T$, and $t1=1$. A transition edge from type $T$ to type $T'$ is a pair $a\\in X_T$, $b\\in X_{T'}$ such that the underlying equal-mass compounds satisfy $a\\prec b$ under [[def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses]]. Thus an edge can exist only when $m(T)=m(T')$. Its cost is the finite real number $\\sigma_{T'}(b)-\\sigma_T(a)$.\n\nA decorated finite chain from $\\Gamma$ to $\\Gamma'$ consists of a catalyst type $C\\in\\mathcal G$, an integer $N\\ge1$, types $T_0=\\Gamma\\times C,T_1,\\ldots,T_N=\\Gamma'\\times C$, and states $a_k\\in X_{T_k}$, $b_{k+1}\\in X_{T_{k+1}}$ with an edge $a_k\\prec b_{k+1}$ for every $0\\le k<N$. Its cost is the finite real sum\n\n$$c=\\sum_{k=0}^{N-1}\\bigl(\\sigma_{T_{k+1}}(b_{k+1})-\\sigma_{T_k}(a_k)\\bigr).$$\n\nDefine the ordered extended real line $\\overline{\\mathbb R}=\\mathbb R\\cup\\{-\\infty,+\\infty\\}$ by placing $-\\infty$ below every real and $+\\infty$ above every real. Finite addition retains its usual meaning; a real added to either infinity leaves that infinity, and equal-sign infinities add to themselves. Opposite-sign infinite sums are undefined. Positive multiplication preserves each infinity, and negation exchanges the infinities. For a nonempty real set bounded below, its infimum is its greatest real lower bound; completeness ensures existence. Define $F:\\mathcal G\\times\\mathcal G\\to\\overline{\\mathbb R}$ by taking $F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma\\prime)$ as the infimum of all such witness costs. Set $F=+\\infty$ if there is no witness and $F=-\\infty$ if the witness costs are unbounded below. The infimum is over real witness costs, so no subtraction of extended-real infima is used. Type permutations, reassociation, reversible identifications, and the identity $T\\times1=T$ are part of the declared type structure. The cost identities and inequalities require a separate proof.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-tdmath-decorated-cost-algebra",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For the cost $F$ of [[def-tdmath-decorated-chain-costs]], $F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma)=0$, $F$ is positively homogeneous, invariant under adding the same catalyst to both endpoints, and satisfies the triangle inequality whenever the extended-real sum on its right is defined. It is subadditive under composition whenever $F(\\Gamma_1,\\Gamma'_1)+F(\\Gamma_2,\\Gamma'_2)$ is defined. Every pair of actual forward and reverse decorated-chain witnesses has nonnegative total cost. A finite reverse witness therefore bounds all forward costs below; $F=-\\infty$ can occur only when the reverse direction has no witness.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-tdmath-forward-spectator-cancellation",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Under A1–A6 of [[def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses]], for compounds $A,B$ with $m(A)=m(B)$ and every finite spectator compound $C$, $A+C\\prec B+C$ if and only if $A\\prec B$. Consequently, for finite-support signed maps $u,v$ of equal total coefficient, define $u\\preceq_s v$ by $(v-u)^-\\prec(v-u)^+$. This relation is well-defined, agrees with $\\prec$ on nonnegative maps, is invariant under adding a common signed map, is transitive, and is closed under addition of two comparisons. No comparison hypothesis is used.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "lem-tdmath-reciprocal-naturals-tend-zero",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "In the complete ordered field $\\mathbb R$, the sequence $\\epsilon_n:=1/(n+1)$, $n\\in\\mathbb N$, is positive, strictly decreasing, and converges to zero.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "If $x\\prec y$, the one-edge witness with an identity catalyst has cost $\\sigma_{\\Gamma'}(y)-\\sigma_\\Gamma(x)$. Hence $F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma')\\le\\sigma_{\\Gamma'}(y)-\\sigma_\\Gamma(x)$, which is the forward inequality, including the case $F=+\\infty$ where no such transition can exist.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "step": "1.2",
          "claim": "Conversely assume the displayed entropy inequality. It is impossible when $F=+\\infty$. Fix an arbitrary natural index $n\\in\\mathbb N$, use $\\epsilon_n$ from [F5] and choose a decorated witness of real cost $c_n\\le\\Delta\\sigma+h\\epsilon_n$, where $\\Delta\\sigma=\\sigma_{\\Gamma'}(y)-\\sigma_\\Gamma(x)$ and $h=\\sigma(z_1)-\\sigma(z_0)>0$ is the entropy gap of a fixed strict meter pair from [F1]. Such witnesses exist from the definition of infimum, also if $F=-\\infty$. Write their types as $T_0=\\Gamma\\times C,T_1,\\ldots,T_N=\\Gamma'\\times C$, their edges as $a_k\\prec b_{k+1}$, and their endpoints as $a_0=(\\widetilde x,c_0)$, $b_N=(\\widetilde y,d_0)$.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F5",
            "choose"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "Put $\\delta_n=\\epsilon_n$. In one finite compound type form\n$L_n=(x,b_N,b_1,\\ldots,b_{N-1},\\delta_n z_0),\\qquad R_n=(a_0,y,a_1,\\ldots,a_{N-1},\\delta_n z_1).$\nThese have the same composition: both contain $\\Gamma$, $\\Gamma'$, the catalyst $C$, every intermediate type $T_1,\\ldots,T_{N-1}$, and one scaled meter state, up to reassociation and permutation. Additivity of $\\sigma$ and the definition of chain cost give\n$\\sigma(R_n)-\\sigma(L_n)=\\Delta\\sigma-c_n+\\delta_n h\\ge0.$\nThus the same-composition entropy representation [F1] gives $L_n\\prec R_n$.",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "F5",
            "step 1.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "Starting from $R_n$, use consistency on $a_0\\prec b_1$ with $y,\\delta_nz_1,a_1,\\ldots,a_{N-1}$ as spectators. The result contains $b_1$ in place of $a_0$. Next use $a_1\\prec b_2$ with $y,\\delta_nz_1,b_1,a_2,\\ldots,a_{N-1}$ as spectators, and continue in order. At stage $k$, the current compound contains $a_k$, the earlier outputs $b_1,\\ldots,b_k$, all later inputs $a_{k+1},\\ldots,a_{N-1}$, and the unchanged $y,\\delta_nz_1$. After the last edge, transitivity gives $R_n\\prec R'_n$, where $R'_n=(b_1,\\ldots,b_N,y,\\delta_nz_1)$. Compose this with $L_n\\prec R_n$ from step 2.1. The compounds $L_n$ and $R'_n$ share the finite spectator $b_1+\\cdots+b_N$; cancel it by [F4]. The result is $x+\\delta_nz_0\\prec y+\\delta_nz_1$ for this $n$. Since $n$ was arbitrary, this comparison holds for every natural index; the chain witnesses need not be chosen simultaneously.",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F4",
            "step 2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "step": "4.1",
          "claim": "By [F5], $\\delta_n\\downarrow0$, stability in [A1] with bases $x,y$ and fixed positive spectators $z_0,z_1$ yields $x\\prec y$. This proves the reverse implication without assuming that any infimum is attained.",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F5",
            "step 3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement empty unit states are the declared singleton of the cost definition. Identity access supplies a zero-cost witness there; where a genuine chain is absent F=+infinity makes the asserted inference false on both sides.",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Statement",
            "source_excerpt": "Assume the calibrated entropy family of [[thm-tdmath-arbitrary-family-calibration]], the cross-type accessibility axioms A1–A6 of [[def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses]], and the decorated cost $F$ of [[def-tdmath-decorated-chain-costs]]. For every pair of types $\\Gamma,\\Gamma'$ with $m(\\Gamma)=m(\\Gamma')$, and states $x\\in X_\\Gamma$, $y\\in X_{\\Gamma'}$,\n\n$$x\\prec y\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad \\sigma_\\Gamma(x)+F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma')\\le \\sigma_{\\Gamma'}(y).$$\n\nThe infimum defining $F$ need not be attained. If $F=+\\infty$, the right side is false; if $F=-\\infty$, the right side is true.",
            "current_raw_sha256": "abcf404cb2672cdd44f2aeb32f38af96f5569c5b51b645358a44421a10f96027",
            "scope_only_not_proof_acceptance": true
          }
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "The positive meter scale tends to zero for stability (step 4.1)."
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
