          "evidence": "A one-edge identity-catalyst witness proves the forward bound (step 1.1)."
        },
        {
          "case": "degenerate",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "The F=+∞ and F=−∞ cases are separated (step 2.1)."
        },
        {
          "case": "endpoints",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Approximate witnesses suffice when the infimum is unattained (step 2.1)."
        },
        {
          "case": "nonempty-choice",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "A witness within tolerance is selected for each n (step 2.1)."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-forward",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Accessibility implies the entropy-cost inequality (step 1.1)."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-reverse",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Approximate costs and stability prove the converse (step 4.1)."
        }
      ],
      "risk_review": {
        "status": "complete",
        "reviewer": "alpha-batch-6",
        "notes": "Reviewed all five steps and both implication directions. The public comparison is now expressly restricted to equal type masses, its actual domain. F=+infinity excludes witnesses, F=-infinity admits any finite threshold. For an arbitrary index choose only one approximating finite witness. Same-composition input/output multiset matching gives L_n prec R_n from its entropy difference; running all actual edges and cancelling their finite output spectator gives x+epsilon z0 prec y+epsilon z1. Universal generalization yields every index without a countable selection, and stability with fixed positive equal-mass meter states closes the reverse implication. Neither an attained infimum nor empirical precision is claimed. Only the offset theorem is a direct in-batch result consumer, and its admitted transitions already have equal mass."
      }
    },
    "def-tdmath-cost-offset-difference-space": {
      "citations": [],
      "derivations": [],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "For the type set $\\mathcal G$ and cost $F$ of [[def-tdmath-decorated-chain-costs]], define the relation $\\Gamma\\approx_0\\Gamma'$ by $F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma')=F(\\Gamma',\\Gamma)=0$. Boundary empty: The item makes no empty boundary claim; this case does not apply to its stated result.",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Definition",
            "source_excerpt": "For the type set $\\mathcal G$ and cost $F$ of [[def-tdmath-decorated-chain-costs]], define the relation $\\Gamma\\approx_0\\Gamma'$ by $F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma')=F(\\Gamma',\\Gamma)=0$. Let $\\mathcal P:=\\mathcal G\\times\\mathcal G$ be the set of raw type pairs. Define a relation on raw pairs by\n\n$$(A,B)\\equiv(A',B')\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\\exists C\\text{ such that }A\\times B'\\times C\\approx_0 A'\\times B\\times C.$$\n\nThe separate well-definedness lemma proves that $\\approx_0$ is an equivalence compatible with composition and positive scaling, that $\\equiv$ is an equivalence on $\\mathcal P$, and that the following operations are well-defined on its classes. After that proof, write $V_F:=\\mathcal P/\\equiv$. Define addition by $[(A,B)]+[(D,E)]:=[(A\\times D,B\\times E)]$, negation by $-[(A,B)]:=[(B,A)]$, and positive scaling by $t[(A,B)]:=[(tA,tB)]$. Set zero to $[(1,1)]$, where $1$ is the empty unit type. The same lemma establishes that these operations make $V_F$ a real vector space; no vector-space law is assumed by this Definition.",
            "current_raw_sha256": "54c5c7038b9ea0703d58d32a898b9489b417e1fc32b6e629b9dd56a3ecc8da27",
            "scope_only_not_proof_acceptance": true
          }
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "For the type set $\\mathcal G$ and cost $F$ of [[def-tdmath-decorated-chain-costs]], define the relation $\\Gamma\\approx_0\\Gamma'$ by $F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma')=F(\\Gamma',\\Gamma)=0$. Boundary zero: The item makes no zero boundary claim; this case does not apply to its stated result.",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Definition",
            "source_excerpt": "For the type set $\\mathcal G$ and cost $F$ of [[def-tdmath-decorated-chain-costs]], define the relation $\\Gamma\\approx_0\\Gamma'$ by $F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma')=F(\\Gamma',\\Gamma)=0$. Let $\\mathcal P:=\\mathcal G\\times\\mathcal G$ be the set of raw type pairs. Define a relation on raw pairs by\n\n$$(A,B)\\equiv(A',B')\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\\exists C\\text{ such that }A\\times B'\\times C\\approx_0 A'\\times B\\times C.$$\n\nThe separate well-definedness lemma proves that $\\approx_0$ is an equivalence compatible with composition and positive scaling, that $\\equiv$ is an equivalence on $\\mathcal P$, and that the following operations are well-defined on its classes. After that proof, write $V_F:=\\mathcal P/\\equiv$. Define addition by $[(A,B)]+[(D,E)]:=[(A\\times D,B\\times E)]$, negation by $-[(A,B)]:=[(B,A)]$, and positive scaling by $t[(A,B)]:=[(tA,tB)]$. Set zero to $[(1,1)]$, where $1$ is the empty unit type. The same lemma establishes that these operations make $V_F$ a real vector space; no vector-space law is assumed by this Definition.",
            "current_raw_sha256": "54c5c7038b9ea0703d58d32a898b9489b417e1fc32b6e629b9dd56a3ecc8da27",
            "scope_only_not_proof_acceptance": true
          }
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "For the type set $\\mathcal G$ and cost $F$ of [[def-tdmath-decorated-chain-costs]], define the relation $\\Gamma\\approx_0\\Gamma'$ by $F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma')=F(\\Gamma',\\Gamma)=0$. Boundary one: The item makes no one boundary claim; this case does not apply to its stated result.",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Definition",
            "source_excerpt": "For the type set $\\mathcal G$ and cost $F$ of [[def-tdmath-decorated-chain-costs]], define the relation $\\Gamma\\approx_0\\Gamma'$ by $F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma')=F(\\Gamma',\\Gamma)=0$. Let $\\mathcal P:=\\mathcal G\\times\\mathcal G$ be the set of raw type pairs. Define a relation on raw pairs by\n\n$$(A,B)\\equiv(A',B')\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\\exists C\\text{ such that }A\\times B'\\times C\\approx_0 A'\\times B\\times C.$$\n\nThe separate well-definedness lemma proves that $\\approx_0$ is an equivalence compatible with composition and positive scaling, that $\\equiv$ is an equivalence on $\\mathcal P$, and that the following operations are well-defined on its classes. After that proof, write $V_F:=\\mathcal P/\\equiv$. Define addition by $[(A,B)]+[(D,E)]:=[(A\\times D,B\\times E)]$, negation by $-[(A,B)]:=[(B,A)]$, and positive scaling by $t[(A,B)]:=[(tA,tB)]$. Set zero to $[(1,1)]$, where $1$ is the empty unit type. The same lemma establishes that these operations make $V_F$ a real vector space; no vector-space law is assumed by this Definition.",
