            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "Suppose $(A,B)\\equiv(A',B')$ via $C$, so $A\\times B'\\times C\\approx_0 A'\\times B\\times C$. For an arbitrary pair $(D,E)$, multiply this witness by $D\\times E$. Reassociation gives $(A\\times D)\\times(B'\\times E)\\times C\\approx_0(A'\\times D)\\times(B\\times E)\\times C$, exactly the witness for $(A\\times D,B\\times E)\\equiv(A'\\times D,B'\\times E)$. Apply the same argument to a change of representative of $(D,E)$ and use transitivity; addition is representative-independent. Scaling a catalyst witness by $t>0$ and using product compatibility from step 1.1 proves that positive scalar multiplication is representative-independent. Product commutativity and associativity give the additive laws, and the empty type gives $(A\\times1,B\\times1)=(A,B)$. Swapping a pair gives an inverse: its sum with $(A,B)$ is $(A\\times B,B\\times A)$, equivalent to $(1,1)$ with unit catalyst because the two product types agree up to permutation and have zero cost in both directions. Thus the classes form an abelian group with zero $[(1,1)]$.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "step": "4.1",
          "claim": "For $t>0$, the displayed operation $t(A,B)=(tA,tB)$ satisfies $t(v+w)=tv+tw$ because scaling distributes over type composition, and $t(sv)=(ts)v$ for $s>0$ by associativity of type scaling. Also $1v=v$. The scalar-addition law for positive $s,t$ follows from step 1.2:\n$(s+t)[A,B]=[(s+t)A,(s+t)B]=[sA\\times tA,sB\\times tB]=s[A,B]+t[A,B].$\nThe zero scalar is defined as the additive identity, and $(-t)v$ is defined as $t(-v)$, where $-v$ swaps the pair.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 1.2",
            "step 3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-5-1",
          "step": "5.1",
          "claim": "The positive scalar laws extend to negative scalars by swapping pairs. For the scalar-addition law with opposite signs, write $s-t$ with $s,t\\ge0$. If $s\\ge t$, positive scalar addition gives $sv=(s-t)v+tv$, so cancellation in the additive group gives $(s-t)v=sv-tv$; if $t>s$, apply the same identity to $t=(t-s)+s$ and negate. Cases with zero are immediate. For scalar composition, write each nonzero scalar as its sign times a positive magnitude; the defined action swaps the pair once for each negative sign and scales by the product of magnitudes, so $(rs)v=r(sv)$, with zero cases immediate. For distributivity over vector addition, positive scalars distribute by step 4.1 and a negative scalar distributes by taking the additive inverse of that identity. Together with the abelian-group laws in step 3.1, these are all real-vector-space axioms.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 4.1",
            "step 3.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
