      "risk_review": {
        "status": "complete",
        "reviewer": "alpha-batch-6",
        "notes": "All six steps checked. Triangle/subadditivity gives both relevant costs at most zero; witnesses d<epsilon exist from that upper bound on the infimum, without presupposing the desired zero equality. Closed-cycle nonnegativity then bounds every opposite witness below by -epsilon, so both infima are zero. Splitting yields actual zero-cost edges, hence scalar addition. Catalysts compose to prove raw-pair transitivity; multiplying and scaling their witnesses proves representative-independence. Pair swapping supplies inverses. Positive scalar laws extend by group cancellation and signs to every real scalar, including zero. The closure and zero-unit conventions in the repaired cost definition support each operation. No Choice is needed beyond that already adopted for the calibrated family."
