    "thm-tdmath-splitting-cost-quotient": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> ([[def-choice-function]]).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-tdmath-cost-offset-difference-space",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For the type set $\\mathcal G$ and cost $F$ of [[def-tdmath-decorated-chain-costs]], define the relation $\\Gamma\\approx_0\\Gamma'$ by $F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma')=F(\\Gamma',\\Gamma)=0$. Let $\\mathcal P:=\\mathcal G\\times\\mathcal G$ be the set of raw type pairs. Define a relation on raw pairs by\n\n$$(A,B)\\equiv(A',B')\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\\exists C\\text{ such that }A\\times B'\\times C\\approx_0 A'\\times B\\times C.$$\n\nThe separate well-definedness lemma proves that $\\approx_0$ is an equivalence compatible with composition and positive scaling, that $\\equiv$ is an equivalence on $\\mathcal P$, and that the following operations are well-defined on its classes. After that proof, write $V_F:=\\mathcal P/\\equiv$. Define addition by $[(A,B)]+[(D,E)]:=[(A\\times D,B\\times E)]$, negation by $-[(A,B)]:=[(B,A)]$, and positive scaling by $t[(A,B)]:=[(tA,tB)]$. Set zero to $[(1,1)]$, where $1$ is the empty unit type. The same lemma establishes that these operations make $V_F$ a real vector space; no vector-space law is assumed by this Definition.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "2.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-tdmath-cost-offset-difference-space-well-defined",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For the construction in [[def-tdmath-cost-offset-difference-space]], $\\approx_0$ is an equivalence compatible with product and positive scaling; $V_F$ is a real vector space and its displayed operations are representative-independent.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "2.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-tdmath-decorated-cost-algebra",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For the cost $F$ of [[def-tdmath-decorated-chain-costs]], $F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma)=0$, $F$ is positively homogeneous, invariant under adding the same catalyst to both endpoints, and satisfies the triangle inequality whenever the extended-real sum on its right is defined. It is subadditive under composition whenever $F(\\Gamma_1,\\Gamma'_1)+F(\\Gamma_2,\\Gamma'_2)$ is defined. Every pair of actual forward and reverse decorated-chain witnesses has nonnegative total cost. A finite reverse witness therefore bounds all forward costs below; $F=-\\infty$ can occur only when the reverse direction has no witness.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-tdmath-infinite-no-sinks-sandwich",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $V$ be a real vector space and $p:V\\to\\mathbb R\\cup\\{+\\infty\\}$ satisfy $p(0)=0$, $p(tx)=tp(x)$ for every $t>0$, and $p(x+y)\\le p(x)+p(y)$ in extended-real arithmetic. Assume **no sinks**: $p(x)<+\\infty$ if and only if $p(-x)<+\\infty$. Put $W:=\\{x\\in V:p(x)<+\\infty\\}$. Then $W$ is a linear subspace and there exists a real-linear $L:V\\to\\mathbb R$ such that\n\n$$-p(-x)\\le L(x)\\le p(x)\\qquad(x\\in V),$$\n\nwith $-\\infty\\le r\\le+\\infty$ interpreted in the usual extended order. The result is valid in arbitrary dimension and allows $p=+\\infty$ outside $W$.\n\nFor the conditional offset application, let $V=V_{\\mathcal G}$ from [[def-tdmath-type-offset-vector-space]], let $F:\\mathcal G\\times\\mathcal G\\to\\mathbb R\\cup\\{+\\infty\\}$ satisfy $p([e_\\Gamma]-[e_{\\Gamma'}])=F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma')$, and define $B(\\Gamma):=L([e_\\Gamma])$. Then $B(\\Gamma\\times\\Gamma')=B(\\Gamma)+B(\\Gamma')$, $B(t\\Gamma)=tB(\\Gamma)$ for $t>0$, and\n\n$$-F(\\Gamma',\\Gamma)\\le B(\\Gamma)-B(\\Gamma')\\le F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma').$$\n\nFor each $\\Gamma\\in\\mathcal G$, let $X_\\Gamma$ be a state set and $\\sigma_\\Gamma:X_\\Gamma\\to\\mathbb R$ a baseline state function. If every admitted transition $(\\Gamma,x)\\to(\\Gamma',y)$, with $x\\in X_\\Gamma$ and $y\\in X_{\\Gamma'}$, satisfies $\\sigma_{\\Gamma'}(y)-\\sigma_\\Gamma(x)\\ge F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma')$, then $\\sigma_\\Gamma(x)+B(\\Gamma)$ is nondecreasing across those transitions. The existence or properties of such a cost $p$, including no-sinks, are hypotheses and are not proved by this theorem.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-tdmath-unattained-cost-entropy-inference",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the calibrated entropy family of [[thm-tdmath-arbitrary-family-calibration]], the cross-type accessibility axioms A1–A6 of [[def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses]], and the decorated cost $F$ of [[def-tdmath-decorated-chain-costs]]. For every pair of types $\\Gamma,\\Gamma'$ with $m(\\Gamma)=m(\\Gamma')$, and states $x\\in X_\\Gamma$, $y\\in X_{\\Gamma'}$,\n\n$$x\\prec y\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad \\sigma_\\Gamma(x)+F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma')\\le \\sigma_{\\Gamma'}(y).$$\n\nThe infimum defining $F$ need not be attained. If $F=+\\infty$, the right side is false; if $F=-\\infty$, the right side is true.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "If $F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma')<+\\infty$, no-sinks gives a finite reverse witness of some real cost $c$. Concatenating it with any forward witness makes a closed chain, so [F2] gives forward cost at least $-c$. Thus $F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma')>-\\infty$ whenever it is finite; all cost values are in $\\mathbb R\\cup\\{+\\infty\\}$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F2",
            "given"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "For a pair class $v=[\\Gamma,\\Gamma']\\in V_F$, define $p(v):=F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma')$. Suppose $(\\Gamma,\\Gamma')\\equiv(\\Lambda,\\Lambda')$ via $C$, so $P=\\Gamma\\times\\Lambda'\\times C\\approx_0 Q=\\Lambda\\times\\Gamma'\\times C$. Put $D=\\Gamma'\\times\\Lambda'\\times C$. Since $P\\approx_0 Q$, the triangle inequality gives $F(P,D)\\le F(Q,D)$ and $F(Q,D)\\le F(P,D)$; the costs have no $-\\infty$ by step 1.1, so these inequalities imply equality. Catalyst invariance from [F2] gives $F(P,D)=F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma')$ and $F(Q,D)=F(\\Lambda,\\Lambda')$. Therefore $p$ is independent of the pair representative.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-2",
          "claim": "The identity $F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma)=0$ gives $p(0)=0$. Homogeneity of $F$ gives $p(tv)=tp(v)$ for $t>0$, and its product subadditivity gives $p(v+w)\\le p(v)+p(w)$. The no-sinks condition says $p(v)<+\\infty$ exactly when $p(-v)<+\\infty$. By step 1.1, $p$ has no $-\\infty$ value.",
          "step": "2.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "given",
            "step 1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Apply [F3] to $V_F$ and $p$. It gives a real-linear $L:V_F\\to\\mathbb R$ with $-p(-v)\\le L(v)\\le p(v)$ for all $v$. Put $B(\\Gamma)=L([\\Gamma,1])$, where $1$ is the empty unit type. Since $[\\Gamma\\times\\Gamma',1]=[\\Gamma,1]+[\\Gamma',1]$, $B$ is additive. Since $[t\\Gamma,1]=t[\\Gamma,1]$ (the unit type satisfies $t1=1$), $B(t\\Gamma)=tB(\\Gamma)$ for $t>0$. Also $[\\Gamma,\\Gamma']=[\\Gamma,1]-[\\Gamma',1]$, so applying the sandwich to this pair gives the displayed two-sided cost bound.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "step 2.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "For an admitted transition, [F4] gives $\\sigma_{\\Gamma'}(y)-\\sigma_\\Gamma(x)\\ge F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma')$, while step 3.1 gives $B(\\Gamma)-B(\\Gamma')\\le F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma')$. Subtracting the latter from the former yields\n$[\\sigma_{\\Gamma'}(y)+B(\\Gamma')]-[\\sigma_\\Gamma(x)+B(\\Gamma)]\\ge0.$\nThus the offset entropy is nondecreasing across every admitted transition.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F4",
            "step 3.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
