    "cex-tdmath-bare-contact-interface-incoherent": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-tdmath-thermal-contact-hypotheses",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For two simple domains $\\Gamma_i\\subset\\mathbb R^{n_i+1}$ and $\\Gamma_j\\subset\\mathbb R^{n_j+1}$, write their coordinates $(u_i,v_i)$ and $(u_j,v_j)$. Their thermal-join domain is\n\n$$\\Delta_{ij}:=\\{(u,v_i,v_j):\\exists u_i,u_j,\\ (u_i,v_i)\\in\\Gamma_i,\\ (u_j,v_j)\\in\\Gamma_j,\\ u=u_i+u_j\\}.$$\n\nThe joined simple type is a distinct formal tag with amount function $\\nu_{\\Delta_{ij}}=\\nu_{\\Gamma_i}+\\nu_{\\Gamma_j}$ and weight $w_{\\Delta_{ij}}=w_{\\Gamma_i}+w_{\\Gamma_j}$, as defined in [[def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses]]. Encode the separated pair by $\\mathsf{J}_{\\Gamma_i}(x)+\\mathsf{J}_{\\Gamma_j}(y)$ and a joined coordinate $Z$ by $\\mathsf{J}_{\\Delta_{ij}}(Z)$. Their bookkeeping masses agree, and their aggregate composition functions agree. These encoded maps and their raw tags remain distinct. Positive coordinate-scaled copies use the reversible preparation transport of that definition; nested joins preserve their finite primitive amount function under reassociation and permutation. The empty type contributes the zero map.\n\nWrite $J_{ij}(x,y)$ for the join state with the summed energy coordinate and the unchanged work coordinates. A compound pair $(x,y)$ and $J_{ij}(x,y)$ have the same total energy and work coordinates but are distinct tagged objects. Define $x\\sim_T y$ exactly when $\\mathsf{J}_{\\Gamma_i}(x)+\\mathsf{J}_{\\Gamma_j}(y)$ and $\\mathsf{J}_{\\Delta_{ij}}(J_{ij}(x,y))$ are accessible in both directions. Nested joins use the declared reassociation and permutation identifications.\n\nAssume every finite join is itself a simple domain satisfying [[def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses]]. The contact hypotheses are mathematical assumptions on the common relation: (T1) $(x,y)\\prec J_{ij}(x,y)$; (T2) every join state has at least one reversible split into component states at its stated coordinates, and the equal-state split of any state into positive scaled copies of itself is reversible; (T3) $\\sim_T$ is transitive; (T4) for every simple state $x$ there are states $x_0,x_1$ in its domain with $x_0\\prec\\prec x\\prec\\prec x_1$ and $x_0\\sim_T x_1$; (T5) for every ordered pair of simple systems, every $x$ in the first and every work coordinate $v$ in the second's projection $\\rho(\\Gamma_j):=\\{v:\\exists u,(u,v)\\in\\Gamma_j\\}$, there is a state $y$ with work coordinate $v$ and $x\\sim_T y$.\n\nFor the completed calibrator branch, add (C1) **join coherence**: whenever $x\\sim_T y$, the join state $J_{ij}(x,y)$ is thermally equivalent to each of $x$ and $y$ under the nested-join identifications. The coordinate sum in the definition alone does not assert C1. In later theorem statements, T1–T5 and C1 are explicitly listed as hypotheses; they are not treated as established physical laws.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "Take one simple coordinate set $\\Gamma=\\mathbb R^2$ and a distinct tagged join set\n$\\Delta=\\{(u,v_1,v_2):\\exists u_1,u_2\\in\\mathbb R,\\ u=u_1+u_2\\}=\\mathbb R^3.$\nFor tagged states $x=(u_1,v_1)$ and $y=(u_2,v_2)$, define $J(x,y)=(u_1+u_2,v_1,v_2)$. This satisfies the stated set-level coordinate formula.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "given",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "On the disjoint tagged union $S=\\Gamma\\sqcup\\Delta$, interpret an otherwise unconstrained relation symbol $\\sim_T$ as equality. It is an equivalence relation. For any $x\\in\\Gamma$, $x\\sim_T x$, but $J(x,x)\\in\\Delta$ is a distinct tagged object and hence $J(x,x)\\not\\sim_T x$. Thus C1 fails while the coordinate formula remains satisfied.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra",
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "On the same tagged sets, interpreting $\\sim_T$ as the universal relation instead makes C1 true, without changing $\\Gamma$, $\\Delta$, or $J$. The bare coordinate data therefore do not determine C1. These interpretations are countermodels only to the stated set-data implication; they are not claimed to satisfy T1–T5.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
