    "ex-tdmath-logarithmic-coherence-witness": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $P$ be a set of primitive type labels. A formal type $\\Gamma$ records a finitely supported amount function $\\nu_\\Gamma:P\\to\\mathbb R_{\\ge0}$, not identically zero. Primitive generators have their unit amount function. Positive scaling, separated product, and thermal-join constructors retain distinct formal tags and obey\n\n$$\\nu_{s\\Gamma}=s\\nu_\\Gamma,\\qquad \\nu_{\\Gamma\\otimes\\Gamma'}=\\nu_\\Gamma+\\nu_{\\Gamma'}\\qquad(s>0).$$\n\nSeparated and joined types are distinct constructors even when their amount functions agree. Permutation, reassociation, and scaled-splitting conventions preserve the amount function. Define the positive **bookkeeping weight** $w_\\Gamma:=\\sum_{p\\in P}\\nu_\\Gamma(p)$, a finite sum over support. Thus $w_{s\\Gamma}=s w_\\Gamma$ and every product or join has weight $w_\\Gamma+w_{\\Gamma'}$. The optional empty unit has zero amount function and weight zero; it is represented only by the empty compound, not as a simple chart. These are formal amounts, not masses in kilograms or an empirical conservation law. The functions and pointwise operations use [[def-function-space]]; their finite-support convention and finite sums are those of [[def-tdmath-tagged-scalable-compound]] and [[lem-tdmath-compound-mass-well-defined]].\n\nFor Euclidean vectors use $a\\cdot b=\\sum_j a_jb_j$ and $\\|a\\|=(a\\cdot a)^{1/2}$. Write $B(a,r)=\\{b:\\|b-a\\|<r\\}$. Open means each point has such a ball contained in the set; convex means $(1-t)a+tb$ belongs to the set for all its $a,b$ and $0\\le t\\le1$. In a subspace or subset, use intersections of these balls for the relative topology. A map $P:D\\to\\mathbb R^n$ is locally Lipschitz if each $a\\in D$ has a relative neighborhood $V$ and finite $L\\ge0$ with $\\|P(b)-P(c)\\|\\le L\\|b-c\\|$ for all $b,c\\in V$. A set $D$ is arcwise connected here if each two distinct points are joined by a continuous injective map $\\gamma:[0,1]\\to D$ with those endpoints; singleton and empty sets satisfy this condition vacuously. Continuity uses the indicated Euclidean relative topology. Let $I$ index the declared family of simple types $\\Gamma_i$. For each simple type $\\Gamma$ in that family, let $n_\\Gamma\\ge1$ and let $\\Gamma\\subset\\mathbb R^{n_\\Gamma+1}$ be nonempty, open and convex, with coordinates $x=(u,v)$. The notation for a type and its chart is shared, but each state has a distinct raw tag $(\\Gamma,x)$. Scaled charts are distinct tagged copies $s\\Gamma=\\{sx:x\\in\\Gamma\\}$ with coordinate transport $x\\mapsto sx$; transported raw tags remain distinct from the original raw tags. Let $e_{(\\Gamma,x)}$ be the unit point mass from [[def-tdmath-tagged-scalable-compound]]. The represented state is\n\n$$\\mathsf{J}_\\Gamma(x):=w_\\Gamma e_{(\\Gamma,x)},\\qquad m(\\mathsf{J}_\\Gamma(x))=w_\\Gamma.$$\n\nWrite $\\mathsf{J}(x):=\\mathsf{J}_\\Gamma(x)$ when $x$ already carries its type tag. All state notation in this framework's compound comparisons, including a bare $x$, denotes $\\mathsf{J}_\\Gamma(x)$; a raw unit point mass is always written $e_{(\\Gamma,x)}$. A separated pair is addition of represented states, not a unit product tag. Nonnegative multiples are pointwise and zero terms are omitted. For an encoded compound $c$, its aggregate composition is the finite amount function\n\n$$\\nu(c):=\\sum_{(\\Gamma,x)\\in\\operatorname{supp}(c)}\\frac{c(\\Gamma,x)}{w_\\Gamma}\\nu_\\Gamma;\\qquad m(c)=\\sum_{p\\in P}\\nu(c)(p).$$\n\nFix the formal interpretation $\\Pi(c)$ as the finite separated preparation whose factor at each nonzero raw tag $(\\Gamma,x)$ is the amount $c(\\Gamma,x)/w_\\Gamma$ of that typed state, using the declared coordinate scaling transport. The accessibility relation on coefficient maps is the pullback of the declared relation on these preparations. Merging or splitting repeated copies of the same preparation is reversible; permutation and reassociation preserve the preparation. Thus $\\Pi(\\mathsf{J}_{s\\Gamma}(sx))$ and $\\Pi(s\\mathsf{J}_\\Gamma(x))$ are the same declared scaled preparation and yield\n\n$$\\mathsf{J}_{s\\Gamma}(sx)\\sim s\\mathsf{J}_\\Gamma(x),$$\n\nalthough their raw point masses have different tags and are not equal. This reversible transport commutes with finite composition, nested type constructors, and scaled splitting. It is part of the precise formal scaling interpretation, not a deduction about physical accessibility from coordinate multiplication. A physical use must adopt the stated preparation maps and accessibility assumptions; fixed-composition models compare only preparations with the same $\\nu$, while a declared channel model may additionally compare different compositions of equal $m$.\n\nA comparison $A\\prec B$ is defined only when $m(A)=m(B)$, as in the coefficient-map supplier. Write $A\\sim B$ when both directions hold, and $A\\prec\\prec B$ when only the forward direction holds. On any fixed simple type, dividing every represented state by the same positive constant $w_\\Gamma$ gives its unit-point-mass comparison. Positive scaling and its inverse preserve and reflect order; whenever the required CH and reference hypotheses hold, the unit-state entropy theorem applies after that normalization. If $S$ represents unit-point-mass comparisons, the entropy of a represented state is $w_\\Gamma S(x)$ and the entropy of a finite sum $\\sum_j t_j\\mathsf{J}_\\Gamma(x_j)$ is $\\sum_j t_j w_\\Gamma S(x_j)$. No independent rescaling across different types is assumed without a calibrator.\n\nThe common relation satisfies A1–A6 on all equal-mass finite compounds: (A1) reflexivity; (A2) transitivity; (A3) if $A\\prec B$, then $A+C\\prec B+C$ for every compound $C$; (A4) if $A\\prec B$, then $tA\\prec tB$ for every $t>0$; (A5) $t\\mathsf{J}_\\Gamma(x)$ is equivalent to $s\\mathsf{J}_\\Gamma(x)+(t-s)\\mathsf{J}_\\Gamma(x)$ for $t>0$ and $0<s<t$, with the reversible scaled-copy transport above; (A6) for equal-mass $A,B$, fixed positive equal-mass spectators $Z_0,Z_1$, and every sequence $\\epsilon_n>0$ decreasing to zero, $A+\\epsilon_n Z_0\\prec B+\\epsilon_n Z_1$ for all $n$ implies $A\\prec B$. These are hypotheses, not conclusions. No comparison hypothesis is included here.\n\nFor each $i$ and $x\\in\\Gamma_i$, define the forward sector $A_x:=\\{y\\in\\Gamma_i:\\mathsf{J}_{\\Gamma_i}(x)\\prec\\mathsf{J}_{\\Gamma_i}(y)\\}$. The simple-domain assumptions are: (A7) for $x,y\\in\\Gamma_i$ and $0<t<1$, the encoded compound $t\\mathsf{J}_{\\Gamma_i}(x)+(1-t)\\mathsf{J}_{\\Gamma_i}(y)$ compares to $\\mathsf{J}_{\\Gamma_i}(tx+(1-t)y)$; both have bookkeeping mass $w_{\\Gamma_i}$, and convexity puts the recombined coordinate in the chart; (S1) each $x$ has a strict successor; (S2) $A_x$ has exactly one supporting hyperplane at $x$, expressible as $u-u_x+P_i(x)\\cdot(v-v_x)=0$ with a finite-valued slope map $P_i:\\Gamma_i\\to\\mathbb R^{n_i}$, and $P_i$ is locally Lipschitz; (S3) the boundary of $A_x$ relative to $\\Gamma_i$ is arcwise connected. A supporting hyperplane means that $A_x$ lies in one of its closed half-spaces and meets the plane at $x$. The affine hull is the smallest affine subspace containing a set; relative interior is interior in that affine hull. Closure and boundary are taken in the indicated affine hull or in $\\Gamma_i$, as named; boundary is closure intersected with closure of the complement in that relative topology.\n\nThis definition imposes no physical interpretation, units, or empirical claim. A contact theorem that uses joins must separately assume that each join is a simple domain satisfying these same forward-sector hypotheses.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "1.3",
            "1.4",
            "1.5",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-tdmath-thermal-contact-hypotheses",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For two simple domains $\\Gamma_i\\subset\\mathbb R^{n_i+1}$ and $\\Gamma_j\\subset\\mathbb R^{n_j+1}$, write their coordinates $(u_i,v_i)$ and $(u_j,v_j)$. Their thermal-join domain is\n\n$$\\Delta_{ij}:=\\{(u,v_i,v_j):\\exists u_i,u_j,\\ (u_i,v_i)\\in\\Gamma_i,\\ (u_j,v_j)\\in\\Gamma_j,\\ u=u_i+u_j\\}.$$\n\nThe joined simple type is a distinct formal tag with amount function $\\nu_{\\Delta_{ij}}=\\nu_{\\Gamma_i}+\\nu_{\\Gamma_j}$ and weight $w_{\\Delta_{ij}}=w_{\\Gamma_i}+w_{\\Gamma_j}$, as defined in [[def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses]]. Encode the separated pair by $\\mathsf{J}_{\\Gamma_i}(x)+\\mathsf{J}_{\\Gamma_j}(y)$ and a joined coordinate $Z$ by $\\mathsf{J}_{\\Delta_{ij}}(Z)$. Their bookkeeping masses agree, and their aggregate composition functions agree. These encoded maps and their raw tags remain distinct. Positive coordinate-scaled copies use the reversible preparation transport of that definition; nested joins preserve their finite primitive amount function under reassociation and permutation. The empty type contributes the zero map.\n\nWrite $J_{ij}(x,y)$ for the join state with the summed energy coordinate and the unchanged work coordinates. A compound pair $(x,y)$ and $J_{ij}(x,y)$ have the same total energy and work coordinates but are distinct tagged objects. Define $x\\sim_T y$ exactly when $\\mathsf{J}_{\\Gamma_i}(x)+\\mathsf{J}_{\\Gamma_j}(y)$ and $\\mathsf{J}_{\\Delta_{ij}}(J_{ij}(x,y))$ are accessible in both directions. Nested joins use the declared reassociation and permutation identifications.\n\nAssume every finite join is itself a simple domain satisfying [[def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses]]. The contact hypotheses are mathematical assumptions on the common relation: (T1) $(x,y)\\prec J_{ij}(x,y)$; (T2) every join state has at least one reversible split into component states at its stated coordinates, and the equal-state split of any state into positive scaled copies of itself is reversible; (T3) $\\sim_T$ is transitive; (T4) for every simple state $x$ there are states $x_0,x_1$ in its domain with $x_0\\prec\\prec x\\prec\\prec x_1$ and $x_0\\sim_T x_1$; (T5) for every ordered pair of simple systems, every $x$ in the first and every work coordinate $v$ in the second's projection $\\rho(\\Gamma_j):=\\{v:\\exists u,(u,v)\\in\\Gamma_j\\}$, there is a state $y$ with work coordinate $v$ and $x\\sim_T y$.\n\nFor the completed calibrator branch, add (C1) **join coherence**: whenever $x\\sim_T y$, the join state $J_{ij}(x,y)$ is thermally equivalent to each of $x$ and $y$ under the nested-join identifications. The coordinate sum in the definition alone does not assert C1. In later theorem statements, T1–T5 and C1 are explicitly listed as hypotheses; they are not treated as established physical laws.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "1.5",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-natural-logarithm",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For $x>0$, define $\\log x$ to be the unique real $y$ such that $\\exp(y)=x$. This is well-defined because [[cor-exponential-is-a-bijection-onto-positive-reals]] states that $\\exp:\\mathbb R\\to(0,\\infty)$ is a bijection.\n\nThus $\\log:(0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R$ is the inverse function of $\\exp$. In particular, $\\log(\\exp y)=y$ for every $y\\in\\mathbb R$ and $\\exp(\\log x)=x$ for every $x>0$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.3",
            "1.4",
            "1.5",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-natural-logarithm-laws",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The function $\\log:(0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R$ is continuous and strictly increasing, is onto $\\mathbb R$, and satisfies, for $x,y>0$,\n$$\\log(xy)=\\log x+\\log y,\\qquad \\log(x/y)=\\log x-\\log y,\\qquad \\log(1/x)=-\\log x.$$\nAlso $\\log 1=0$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.3",
            "1.4",
            "1.5",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-logarithm-derivative-and-integral",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For $x>0$, $\\log$ is differentiable and\n$$\\log'(x)=\\frac1x,\\qquad \\log x=\\int_1^x\\frac{dt}{t}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.3",
            "1.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-weighted-am-gm-real",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n\\ge1$, let $x_1,\\ldots,x_n>0$, and let $w_1,\\ldots,w_n\\ge0$ satisfy $\\sum_{i=1}^nw_i=1$. Then\n$$\\prod_{i=1}^n x_i^{w_i}\\le\\sum_{i=1}^nw_ix_i.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-exponential-two-point-convexity",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For all $x,y\\in\\mathbb R$ and $0\\le t\\le1$,\n$$\\exp((1-t)x+ty)\\le(1-t)\\exp x+t\\exp y.$$\nIf $0<t<1$, equality holds exactly when $x=y$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.3"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Fix a finite list of scaled components with amounts $t_j>0$ and labels $i_j\\in I$. Write $\\alpha_j=t_jc_{i_j}$, $\\beta_j=t_jr_{i_j}$, $d_j=t_ju^*_{i_j}$, and $e_j=t_jv^*_{i_j}$, with $C=\\sum_j\\alpha_j>0$. For a split of total energy $U>0$ into positive $U_j$ with $\\sum_jU_j=U$, put $p_j=\\alpha_j/C$ and $r_j=U_j/\\alpha_j$. Then $\\sum_jp_jr_j=U/C$. By [F5] and monotonicity of $\\log$ from [F3],\n$\\sum_j\\alpha_j\\log(U_j/d_j)\n=\\sum_j\\alpha_j\\log(\\alpha_j/d_j)+C\\sum_jp_j\\log r_j\n\\le \\sum_j\\alpha_j\\log(\\alpha_j/d_j)+C\\log(U/C)\n=\\sum_j\\alpha_j\\log(\\alpha_jU/(Cd_j)).$\nEquality in the finite weighted Jensen step occurs only when all $r_j$ are equal: [F6] gives strict concavity for any unequal pair with positive weights, and grouping terms applies this two-point strict inequality inductively. Since the weighted average is $U/C$, equality requires $r_j=U/C$, or $U_j=\\alpha_jU/C$. This allocation is positive and feasible, so the maximum is attained uniquely. The work-coordinate terms do not depend on the energy split, giving exactly the displayed join entropy. Grouping nested joins is the same finite maximization over all $U_j$, so the formula is associative under grouping and permutation.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "On each fixed composition, define accessibility by nondecrease of the displayed entropy. Reflexivity and transitivity follow from the real order. A common spectator adds the same entropy to both sides; positive scaling multiplies each entropy comparison by a positive factor. For a simple scaled type, replacing $(U,V,t)$ by $(sU,sV,st)$ leaves both log ratios unchanged and multiplies their coefficients and constant term by $s$. In a join, scaling every amount, energy, and work coordinate by $s$ likewise leaves $\\alpha_jU/(Cd_j)$ and $V_j/e_j$ unchanged and scales all coefficients and additive constants by $s$. Extensivity gives $\\sigma_{(s+t)i}((s+t)x)=\\sigma_{si}(sx)+\\sigma_{ti}(tx)$, proving reversible splitting. For the stability condition, if $\\sigma(A)+\\epsilon_n\\sigma(Z_0)\\le\\sigma(B)+\\epsilon_n\\sigma(Z_1)$ for all $n$ and $\\epsilon_n\\downarrow0$, the four entropies are finite. Moreover, the premise includes equality of aggregate composition: $\\nu(A)-\\nu(B)=\\epsilon_n(\\nu(Z_1)-\\nu(Z_0))$ for every $n$. Choose two different values of $\\epsilon_n$, which exist because the positive sequence tends to zero; subtracting these equalities gives $\\nu(Z_1)=\\nu(Z_0)$, and then $\\nu(A)=\\nu(B)$. Taking limits in the entropy inequality gives $\\sigma(A)\\le\\sigma(B)$, now with the same-composition condition required by the accessibility definition. This verifies the stated accessibility and stability hypotheses on the declared comparison domains.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-3",
          "claim": "For $x,y\\in\\Gamma_i$ and $0<t<1$, strict concavity of $\\log$ from [F6] gives concavity of $(U,V)\\mapsto c_i\\log(U/u_i^*)+r_i\\log(V/v_i^*)$. Jensen's inequality in each coordinate yields $t\\sigma_i(x)+(1-t)\\sigma_i(y)\\le\\sigma_i(tx+(1-t)y)$, so the convex-combination access condition in [F1] holds. Strict increase of $\\log U$ at fixed $V$ gives a strict successor, proving S1.",
          "step": "1.3",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F6",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-4",
          "claim": "For a simple type of amount $t$, put $\\alpha=tc_i$ and $\\beta=tr_i$. Its forward sector is the superlevel set of $\\alpha\\log U+\\beta\\log V$; the boundary through $(U_0,V_0)$ is the smooth graph $U=U_0(V/V_0)^{-\\beta/\\alpha}$ for $V>0$. By [F4], its tangent slope is $dU/dV=-\\beta U/(\\alpha V)$, so the support plane has the required form with $P(U,V)=\\beta U/(\\alpha V)$. The gradient is nonzero. For any boundary tangent vector, a differentiable boundary curve exists with that tangent; applying a supporting linear inequality to both positive and negative curve parameters forces the support normal to annihilate the tangent space. That space has codimension one and its normal is the nonzero gradient, so the supporting hyperplane is unique. The slope $P$ is smooth and locally Lipschitz. The graph domain $(0,\\infty)$ is arcwise connected, proving S2–S3. For a finite join, replace $\\alpha$ by $C$ and use each positive $\\beta_j$; the boundary graph $U=K\\prod_jV_j^{-\\beta_j/C}$ lies over the connected positive orthant and has locally Lipschitz slopes $P_j=\\beta_jU/(CV_j)$.",
          "step": "1.4",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-5",
          "claim": "For any state $(U,V)$ of amount $t$ and any allowed work coordinate $W>0$ in a second type of amount $t'$, choose $Y=(t'c_jU/(tc_i),W)$. It lies in the positive state domain and satisfies $\\theta_{ti}(U,V)=\\theta_{t'j}(Y)$, verifying T5. For T4, choose $0<V_0<V<V_1$ at fixed $U$. Strict increase in the volume coordinate gives $(U,V_0)\\prec\\prec(U,V)\\prec\\prec(U,V_1)$, while the endpoints have the same $\\theta=U/(tc_i)$ and hence are thermally equivalent.",
          "step": "1.5",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "given",
            "choose"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Step 1.1 shows that joined entropy is the maximum of the separated entropy over all admissible energy splits. Thus every split compares upward to the join, and the maximizing split has equal entropy with the join, so it compares back; this verifies T1–T2. For a pair of simple components, the maximizing allocation is characterized by $U_i/\\alpha_i=U_j/\\alpha_j$, exactly equality of $\\theta_{t_i i}(U_i,V_i):=U_i/(t_ic_i)$ and $\\theta_{t_j j}(U_j,V_j)$. The initial split is reversible with the join exactly when its entropy equals the maximum, which is exactly this equality. Therefore $\\sim_T$ is equality of the scalar label, and transitivity follows.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F5",
            "step 1.1",
            "given"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "If two component states are thermally equivalent, their common label is $\\theta$. At their join, the maximizing split has energies $U_i=t_ic_i\\theta$ and $U_j=t_jc_j\\theta$, so the joined label is $(U_i+U_j)/(t_ic_i+t_jc_j)=\\theta$. The same identity holds after any finite grouping by step 1.1. Therefore the joined state is thermally equivalent to each component, verifying C1 under the declared nested-join identifications. Every logarithm argument is a ratio of like positive quantities, hence dimensionless. The parameter $\\theta$ is only a mathematical contact label; this example asserts no empirical temperature law.",
          "step": "3.1",
